Below is a simple C++ solution. The idea is to check if the given 3-letter string can become "abc" by performing at most one swap. We try all possible pairs of positions to see if after swapping them, the string becomes "abc". If either the string is already "abc" or it can be made "abc" with one such swap, the answer is "YES"; otherwise, it is "NO".

#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int t;
    cin >> t;
    while (t--) {
        string s;
        cin >> s;

        // If it's already "abc", we are done.
        if (s == "abc") {
            cout << "YES\n";
            continue;
        }

        bool possible = false;
        // Try swapping each pair of positions (only 3 positions: 0,1,2).
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < 3; j++) {
                string temp = s;
                swap(temp[i], temp[j]);
                if (temp == "abc") {
                    possible = true;
                    break;
                }
            }
            if (possible) break;
        }

        cout << (possible ? "YES\n" : "NO\n");
    }

    return 0;
}

Below is a general approach to using a Toolbar within a Fragment. Note that Fragments do not directly manage the app’s Toolbar as easily as an Activity does; however, you can still set up a Toolbar inside a Fragment by coordinating with the hosting Activity:

1. Make sure your Activity uses a Theme that supports no built-in ActionBar  
   In your app’s styles.xml, for example:  
   <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.Light.NoActionBar">
       <!-- Customize your theme here. -->
   </style>

2. Include a Toolbar in your Fragment’s layout XML  
   Example (fragment_example.xml):  
   <?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
   <androidx.constraintlayout.widget.ConstraintLayout
       xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
       xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
       xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
       android:layout_width="match_parent"
       android:layout_height="match_parent">

       <androidx.appcompat.widget.Toolbar
           android:id="@+id/myToolbar"
           android:layout_width="match_parent"
           android:layout_height="?attr/actionBarSize"
           android:background="?attr/colorPrimary"
           android:theme="@style/ThemeOverlay.AppCompat.Dark.ActionBar"
           app:layout_constraintTop_toTopOf="parent"
           app:title="My Fragment Toolbar" />

       <!-- The rest of your fragment UI here. -->

   </androidx.constraintlayout.widget.ConstraintLayout>

3. Inflate and set this Toolbar in the Fragment’s onViewCreated (or onCreateView)  
   class ExampleFragment : Fragment() {

       override fun onCreateView(
           inflater: LayoutInflater, 
           container: ViewGroup?, 
           savedInstanceState: Bundle?
       ): View? {
           // Inflate the fragment layout that contains the Toolbar
           return inflater.inflate(R.layout.fragment_example, container, false)
       }

       override fun onViewCreated(view: View, savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
           super.onViewCreated(view, savedInstanceState)

           // Find the toolbar
           val toolbar = view.findViewById<Toolbar>(R.id.myToolbar)

           // Let the hosting Activity handle the setSupportActionBar call
           // Casting to AppCompatActivity is typically needed
           (requireActivity() as AppCompatActivity).setSupportActionBar(toolbar)

           // (Optional) If you want to handle menu items inside this Fragment
           setHasOptionsMenu(true)
       }

       // (Optional) If you set setHasOptionsMenu(true), you can inflate the toolbar menu here:
       override fun onCreateOptionsMenu(menu: Menu, inflater: MenuInflater) {
           inflater.inflate(R.menu.my_fragment_toolbar_menu, menu)
           super.onCreateOptionsMenu(menu, inflater)
       }

       // (Optional) Handle menu item clicks:
       override fun onOptionsItemSelected(item: MenuItem): Boolean {
           return when (item.itemId) {
               R.id.some_menu_item -> {
                   // Handle click
                   true
               }
               else -> super.onOptionsItemSelected(item)
           }
       }
   }

4. Additional notes and common gotchas:  
   • Make sure your Fragment is attached to an AppCompatActivity if you want to use setSupportActionBar().  
   • Use (requireActivity() as AppCompatActivity) or activity as? AppCompatActivity to cast your host Activity.  
   • If you do not need Fragment-specific menu items, you can handle your Toolbar menu in the Activity instead.  
   • If you have an existing App bar or ActionBar in the Activity, make sure the theme or layout does not conflict with the Fragment Toolbar (e.g., use a NoActionBar theme or hide the Activity’s existing ActionBar to avoid duplication).  

With these steps, you can properly add and manage a Toolbar in your Fragment, including creating a custom title, adding menu items, and handling navigation or other actions.

Below is an example of a breadth-first search (BFS) approach in JavaScript that locates an object by its "name" property somewhere inside a nested structure. It avoids recursion by using a queue. In addition to the found object, it returns the path (as an array of keys) used to reach the object.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/**
 * Finds an object that has a "name" property matching the targetName
 * deep inside a given root object, without using recursion.
 * Returns an object with two properties:
 *   - foundObj: reference to the actual object in the tree
 *   - path: an array of keys representing how to reach that object
 *
 * If there is no match, returns null.
 *
 * @param {object} root - The root object to search within.
 * @param {string} targetName - The name value to look for.
 * @returns {{ foundObj: any, path: string[] } | null}
 */
function findObjectByName(root, targetName) {
  // Queue items will be objects of the form:
  // { current: <current node>, path: <array of keys to reach current> }
  const queue = [{ current: root, path: [] }];

  while (queue.length > 0) {
    const { current, path } = queue.shift();

    // Only proceed if the current item is an object or array
    if (current && typeof current === 'object') {
      // Check if this object has the desired name
      if (current.name === targetName) {
        return { foundObj: current, path };
      }

      // Enqueue all child objects
      for (const key in current) {
        // Only queue up properties that belong to current (own properties)
        // and are themselves objects or arrays
        if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(current, key) &&
            current[key] !== null &&
            typeof current[key] === 'object') {
          queue.push({
            current: current[key],
            path: [...path, key]  // Extend the path with the current key
          });
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // If we exhaust the queue without finding the desired object, return null
  return null;
}
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Usage Example:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Example nested object
const myObject = {
  id: 1,
  name: "root",
  child: {
    id: 2,
    name: "level1",
    data: {
      id: 3,
      name: "target",
      extra: "some data"
    }
  },
  otherChild: {
    id: 4,
    nested: {
      id: 5,
      name: "anotherTarget"
    }
  }
};

// Search for the object with name "target"
const result = findObjectByName(myObject, "target");

if (result) {
  console.log("Object found:", result.foundObj);
  console.log("Path:", result.path);
  // For the example above, result.path would be ["child", "data"]
} else {
  console.log("Object with that name not found.");
}
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Explanation:
1. A queue (FIFO) is used to hold objects to be processed.  
2. Each queue entry is an object that stores the current node under inspection (current) and the path of keys (path) taken from the root down to this node.  
3. You check if the current node has the matching "name" property. If so, you return the path and the node.  
4. If not, you enqueue all children that are objects, appending their keys to the path, until you exhaust the queue.  
5. If you reach the end of the queue without a match, you return null.  

This ensures we search the object graph without any recursive function calls, while still preserving and returning the path to the matched object.

