Hello, what do you think of this arduino code in regards to understandability, optimization and size?
Any suggestions for improvements?

void cycleLEDs(int interval) {
  const int nOutPins = sizeof(outPins) / sizeof(outPins[0]);
  static unsigned long lastChange = 0;
  static int currIndex = 0; // Use static to retain value between function calls
  int nextIndex = 0;

  if (millis() >= lastChange + abs(interval)) {

    // Determine direction
    int direction = interval < 0 ? -1 : 1;

    // Update secondary index
    nextIndex = currIndex + direction;
    if (nextIndex < 0) {
      nextIndex = nOutPins - 1;
    } else if (nextIndex >= nOutPins) {
      nextIndex = 0;
    }

    // Turn off all LEDs
    for (int i = 0; i < nOutPins; i++) {
      if (i == currIndex || i == nextIndex){
        digitalWrite(outPins[i], HIGH);
      } else {
        digitalWrite(outPins[i], LOW);
      }      
    }

    // Update current index
    currIndex += direction;
    if (currIndex < 0) {
      currIndex = nOutPins - 1;
    } else if (currIndex >= nOutPins) {
      currIndex = 0;
    }

    // Update timer
    lastChange = millis();
  }
}

Thank you for your help, i value your input.

output:
Below are some observations and suggestions that might help improve your code’s clarity, efficiency, and size. Overall, your code is already quite readable and understandable; most of these suggestions are optional tweaks for style or micro-optimizations.

─────────────────────────────────────────────────
1) Clarify the “direction” logic and wrapping
─────────────────────────────────────────────────

• Use the modulo operator (%) to wrap the LED index around instead of doing manual if/else for negative or out-of-bounds. Using a wrap function or a direct modulo expression makes the code shorter and often clearer:

  // Helper function
  int wrapIndex(int idx, int size) {
    // Ensures index always stays between [0, size-1]
    return (idx + size) % size;
  }

Then you can do:

  int direction = (interval < 0) ? -1 : 1;
  nextIndex = wrapIndex(currIndex + direction, nOutPins);

And similarly, after you update currIndex:

  currIndex = wrapIndex(currIndex + direction, nOutPins);

This approach can eliminate several lines of boundary-checking code, reducing chances for off-by-one errors.

─────────────────────────────────────────────────
2) Simplify turning LEDs on/off
─────────────────────────────────────────────────

• If you only need two LEDs on (currIndex and nextIndex) and all others off, you can write them in one pass:

  for (int i = 0; i < nOutPins; ++i) {
    digitalWrite(outPins[i], (i == currIndex || i == nextIndex) ? HIGH : LOW);
  }

Alternatively, if you only want to change the LEDs that actually need changing (to save some cycles), you could turn off the old index and turn on the new one—but that’s only beneficial if you’re certain about the previous state of each LED. The all-off/all-on approach is often safer and still quite clear.

─────────────────────────────────────────────────
3) Consistent timing check
─────────────────────────────────────────────────

• Many Arduino users prefer checking intervals with a subtraction approach:

  if (millis() - lastChange >= (unsigned long)abs(interval)) {
    lastChange = millis();
    // Do LED updates...
  }

This helps avoid potential overflow pitfalls with (millis() >= lastChange + abs(interval)), especially if you’re dealing with larger intervals or have the board running for a long time. It also makes it more explicit that you’re checking the time elapsed since lastChange.

─────────────────────────────────────────────────
4) Make “interval” unsigned if negative is only for direction
─────────────────────────────────────────────────

• If the only reason interval can be negative is to determine direction, consider changing your function signature and splitting out “speed” and “direction” as separate parameters. For example:

  void cycleLEDs(unsigned long interval, bool reverseDirection) {
    int direction = reverseDirection ? -1 : 1;
    // ...
  }

That will let you remove abs(interval) and may clarify logic. However, if you intentionally use negative intervals as a quick way to set direction, that’s fine—just be consistent so others (and your future self) understand it easily.

─────────────────────────────────────────────────
5) Keep static variables obvious
─────────────────────────────────────────────────

• Make sure it’s clear to future readers that lastChange and currIndex are meant to persist between calls. Perhaps add a short comment explaining their use, or consider giving them more descriptive names:

  static unsigned long s_lastChange = 0; // Tracks time of last LED cycle
  static int s_currIndex = 0;           // Current lit LED index

This doesn’t affect size or performance much, but strengthens readability.

─────────────────────────────────────────────────
6) Final thoughts
─────────────────────────────────────────────────

• In terms of code size, the differences among these suggestions usually come down to a few bytes at most (often overshadowed by other library overhead). The primary gain is clearer logic and a slightly more compact way to handle edge cases.

• If you’re optimizing for minimal code size, you can inline or reduce calls (e.g., compute direction inline, use a single pass for LED writes), but typically readability is more important unless you’re very tight on flash memory.

In summary:  
• Your logic is already straightforward and functional.  
• Switching to a wrapIndex approach or direct modulo will shorten and clarify code.  
• Using the subtraction-based timing check (millis() - lastChange >= duration) is often the recommended pattern.  
• Separating “interval” from “direction” can make the code more logical, but isn’t strictly necessary.

Hope those tips help! If you have any more questions or want a specific modification, feel free to ask.

