Below is a simple example of a PHP script that connects to a MySQL database called "Bob," accepts “field1” and “field2” as HTTP POST parameters, and inserts them into a table. This example uses mysqli with prepared statements for added security (‘?’ placeholders with bind_param to avoid SQL injection).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Example script: insert_data.php
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
<?php
// Database connection parameters
$servername = "localhost";  // or the IP address/hostname of your DB server
$username   = "your_username";
$password   = "your_password";
$database   = "Bob";        // Name of the database

// Create connection
$conn = new mysqli($servername, $username, $password, $database);

// Check connection
if ($conn->connect_error) {
    die("Connection failed: " . $conn->connect_error);
}

// Safely retrieve POST parameters (handle if they are not set)
$field1 = isset($_POST['field1']) ? $_POST['field1'] : '';
$field2 = isset($_POST['field2']) ? $_POST['field2'] : '';

// Prepare an SQL statement (example table name: my_table)
$stmt = $conn->prepare("INSERT INTO my_table (field1, field2) VALUES (?, ?)");
if (!$stmt) {
    die("Prepare failed: " . $conn->error);
}

// Bind the parameters to the SQL query
$stmt->bind_param("ss", $field1, $field2);

// Execute the statement
if ($stmt->execute()) {
    echo "Data inserted successfully!";
} else {
    echo "Error inserting data: " . $stmt->error;
}

// Close statement and connection
$stmt->close();
$conn->close();
?>

Instructions to use:
1. Make sure you have a database named "Bob" in your MySQL server.  
2. Create a table named "my_table" with columns “id” (AUTO_INCREMENT primary key), “field1” (VARCHAR or any suitable type), and “field2” (also VARCHAR or any suitable type). For example:
   CREATE TABLE my_table (
       id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
       field1 VARCHAR(255),
       field2 VARCHAR(255)
   );
3. Modify the database credentials ($servername, $username, $password) in the script.  
4. Place the insert_data.php file on your web server with PHP enabled.  
5. Send an HTTP POST request to insert_data.php (e.g., using an HTML form or a tool such as cURL) with the fields field1 and field2. For example, an HTML form:

   <form action="insert_data.php" method="post">
       <label for="field1">Field 1:</label>
       <input type="text" name="field1" id="field1">
       <label for="field2">Field 2:</label>
       <input type="text" name="field2" id="field2">
       <button type="submit">Submit</button>
   </form>

When the form is submitted (or any POST request is made with the correct parameter names), the values of field1 and field2 will be inserted into the “my_table” inside the “Bob” database.

Below is a minimal example of a Chrome extension (Manifest V3) that, when you click its toolbar icon, retrieves the current page’s HTML and automatically downloads it as a file called “page.html”.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1) manifest.json
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
{
  "name": "Save Page Content",
  "description": "Saves the content of the current page as an HTML file.",
  "version": "1.0",
  "manifest_version": 3,
  "permissions": [
    "activeTab",
    "scripting",
    "downloads"
  ],
  "background": {
    "service_worker": "background.js"
  },
  "action": {
    "default_title": "Save current page"
  }
}

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2) background.js
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Listen for a click on the extension's toolbar icon.
chrome.action.onClicked.addListener((tab) => {
  // Run a function in the context of the current page to get its HTML.
  chrome.scripting.executeScript(
    {
      target: { tabId: tab.id },
      func: getPageHTML
    },
    (results) => {
      if (chrome.runtime.lastError) {
        console.error(chrome.runtime.lastError);
        return;
      }

      // The content script returns an array with the result from each frame;
      // we'll use results[0].result for the top frame’s content.
      const html = results[0].result;

      // Trigger a file download with the extracted HTML.
      chrome.downloads.download({
        url: URL.createObjectURL(
          new Blob([html], { type: "text/html" })
        ),
        filename: "page.html"
      });
    }
  );
});

// Function injected into the current page to get the outer HTML of the document.
function getPageHTML() {
  return document.documentElement.outerHTML;
}

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Installation Instructions
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Create a folder (e.g., save-page-extension/).  
2. Place manifest.json and background.js in that folder.  
3. Go to chrome://extensions in your Chrome browser.  
4. Enable “Developer Mode” (toggle in the top-right corner).  
5. Click “Load unpacked” and select your folder.  
6. The extension’s icon should appear in the toolbar.  
7. Navigate to any webpage and click your new extension icon to download the HTML content.  

You’ve now built a simple Chrome extension that can save the current page’s HTML with a single click!

