Write functionality to print the rxdataF variable in c:
ru->common.rxdataF     = (int32_t**)malloc16(ru->nb_rx*sizeof(int32_t*) );
ru->common.rxdataF[i] = (int32_t*)malloc16_clear(sizeof(int32_t)*(NUMBER_RX_BUFFERS*fp->symbols_per_slot*fp->ofdm_symbol_size) );

output:
Below is an example C function that prints out the contents of ru->common.rxdataF assuming each 32-bit entry holds two 16-bit values (real then imaginary). You may adjust the loop bounds or print format to suit your specific use case. The example also assumes you have access to the RU structure (ru), the FrameParms structure (fp), and the NUMBER_RX_BUFFERS definition.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

// Example function to print rxdataF
void print_rxdataF(const RU_t *ru, const FrameParms *fp) {
    // Each element of rxdataF[i] is an int32_t containing two int16_t values:
    //    lower 16 bits = real part
    //    upper 16 bits = imaginary part
    for (int rxAntenna = 0; rxAntenna < ru->nb_rx; rxAntenna++) {
        printf("Rx Data for antenna %d:\n", rxAntenna);

        int total_samples = NUMBER_RX_BUFFERS * fp->symbols_per_slot * fp->ofdm_symbol_size;

        for (int sampleIdx = 0; sampleIdx < total_samples; sampleIdx++) {
            // Extract real and imaginary parts
            int32_t val = ru->common.rxdataF[rxAntenna][sampleIdx];
            int16_t re  = (int16_t)( val        & 0xFFFF);  // lower 16 bits
            int16_t im  = (int16_t)((val >> 16) & 0xFFFF);  // upper 16 bits

            printf("Sample %5d: %6d + j%6d\n", sampleIdx, re, im);
        }
    }
}
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Explanation:
• ru->common.rxdataF is allocated as an array of pointers, each pointing to an int32_t buffer.
• In a typical LTE/NR baseband implementation (as seen in the OpenAirInterface codebase), each int32_t represents a complex sample with separate 16-bit real and 16-bit imaginary components.  
• The lower 16 bits (mask 0xFFFF) store the real part; the upper 16 bits (shift right by 16) store the imaginary part.  
• Adjust the loop range (NUMBER_RX_BUFFERS, symbols_per_slot, and ofdm_symbol_size) as needed for your scenario.  
• Each iteration of the inner loop prints the sample index and the real/imag values in decimal.  

With these changes, you will be able to print the contents of rxdataF for debugging or analysis.

