Below is one way to make the original logic clearer by removing the low-level bit-manipulation and 0xFFFFFFFF “length” trick. The code below illustrates the same behavior in a more familiar C style:

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/* Globals (assume they exist) */
int IsWindowedMode;
extern unsigned int RK_CheckSJIS(unsigned char ch);

/* Improved, more readable version */
void handleCmdLineArgs(char *param)
{
    /* 1. Compute the length of the input string */
    int length = 0;
    while (param[length] != '\0') {
        length++;
    }

    /*
     * 2. If the string length is greater than 1, 
     *    scan through and handle SHIFT-JIS pairs 
     *    and the "/w" or "/f" arguments.
     */
    if (length > 1) {
        int i = 0;
        while (i < (length - 1)) {
            /*
             * Check if current character is part of a 2-byte SJIS sequence.
             * If RK_CheckSJIS() returns 1, it means we should skip the next char 
             * because the current char and the next char together form a 2-byte SJIS code.
             */
            if (RK_CheckSJIS((unsigned char)param[i]) == 1) {
                i++;  /* skip the next character */
            }
            else if (param[i] == '/') {
                /*
                 * If we see a slash, look at the following character,
                 * force it to lowercase (via OR 0x20), 
                 * and toggle IsWindowedMode accordingly.
                 */
                char c = param[i + 1];
                c = (char)(c | 0x20);  /* convert to lowercase */

                if (c == 'w') {
                    IsWindowedMode = 1;   /* /w => windowed */
                }
                else if (c == 'f') {
                    IsWindowedMode = 0;   /* /f => fullscreen */
                }
            }

            i++;
        }
    }
}

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Explanation of key changes:

• We first compute the length of the string by a simple loop instead of the original 0xFFFFFFFF decrement approach.  
• Instead of using the bit-mask 0xFFFFFF00 to insert a single character into uVar2, we pass param[i] directly as an unsigned char into RK_CheckSJIS().  
• If RK_CheckSJIS() returns 1, that indicates the current and subsequent character form a 2-byte SJIS sequence, so we increment the index again to skip the extra byte.  
• When we see a slash '/', we peek at the next character, lowercase it (by OR-ing with 0x20), and decide if it means “windowed” or “fullscreen.”  

This maintains the same overall logic and control flow in a more typical high-level C style.

To align two buttons to opposite sides of a parent container using Flexbox (for responsiveness), you can do something like this:

