Below is a simple example using the Leibniz series to approximate π in JavaScript. The series converges slowly but demonstrates the concept clearly. You can adjust the “iterations” value to trade off speed for precision.

-------------------------------------------------------
Example: Approximating π using the Leibniz formula
-------------------------------------------------------

/*
  The Leibniz formula for π is:
     π = 4 * (1 - 1/3 + 1/5 - 1/7 + 1/9 - 1/11 + ... )

  We can compute an approximation by summing a finite number
  of terms in this alternating series.
*/

function approximatePi(iterations) {
  let piOverFour = 0;
  let denominator = 1;

  for (let i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
    // Add or subtract the term based on whether i is even or odd
    if (i % 2 === 0) {
      piOverFour += 1 / denominator;
    } else {
      piOverFour -= 1 / denominator;
    }
    denominator += 2;
  }

  return 4 * piOverFour;
}

// Specify how many terms of the series to use
const iterations = 1000000; // e.g., 1 million
const piApproximation = approximatePi(iterations);

console.log(`Using ${iterations} iterations, π is approximately: ${piApproximation}`);

-------------------------------------------
Usage and Tips:
-------------------------------------------
1. Save the code as “approximatePi.js” or similar.  
2. Run it with node (e.g., node approximatePi.js) or in your browser console.  
3. Increase “iterations” for a more accurate approximation (but it will take longer).

Below is a simple C# console application that computes π to five decimal places, converts it to an integer form, and then demonstrates XORing that integer twice with the same key (which returns the original value). Finally, it converts back to a double to show the recovered π:

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Program.cs
using System;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        // 1. Compute π to 5 decimal places
        double pi = Math.Round(Math.PI, 5);
        Console.WriteLine("π to 5 decimals: " + pi);

        // 2. Convert to an integer by shifting the decimal point
        int piAsInt = (int)(pi * 100000);
        Console.WriteLine("As integer (multiplied by 100000): " + piAsInt);

        // 3. Define an XOR key
        int key = 12345;

        // 4. XOR the integer once
        int xorOnce = piAsInt ^ key;
        Console.WriteLine("XOR once: " + xorOnce);

        // 5. XOR the result again (with the same key)
        int xorTwice = xorOnce ^ key;
        Console.WriteLine("XOR twice: " + xorTwice);

        // 6. Convert the double-XOR result back to a double
        double recoveredPi = xorTwice / 100000.0;
        Console.WriteLine("Recovered π: " + recoveredPi);
    }
}
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Explanation:
1) Math.Round(Math.PI, 5) computes π with five decimal places.  
2) Multiplying by 100000 and casting to int shifts the decimal digits so they can be XORed easily.  
3) XORing the same value with the same key twice undoes the XOR (it returns the original).  
4) Dividing by 100000.0 shifts the decimal place back to recover the double-precision value.

Below is a minimal example showing how you can build a simple BlenderBot chat application in pure vanilla JavaScript using Transformers.js. Note that:

• The facebook/blenderbot-400M-distill model is fairly large (hundreds of MB), so loading may take a while (and use significant memory) if run entirely in the browser.  
• This example uses the “text2text-generation” pipeline rather than a built-in “conversational” pipeline, since Transformers.js does not yet offer a dedicated conversation pipeline. We’ll simulate conversation by manually storing chat history.  
• Make sure you serve your HTML file from a local server or hosting environment (browsers often block large model downloads or certain worker operations if opened via file://).

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

1) Set up your HTML

Create a file called index.html with this simple structure:

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <title>BlenderBot Chat</title>
  <!-- Load Transformers.js from a CDN. 
       Check https://github.com/xenova/transformers.js for the latest URL. -->
  <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@xenova/transformers@3.5.0/dist/transformers.min.js"></script>
  <style>
    body {
      font-family: sans-serif;
      margin: 1.5rem;
    }
    #chat-box {
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 600px;
      margin: 0 auto 1.5rem auto;
      border: 1px solid #ccc;
      padding: 1rem;
      min-height: 300px;
      overflow-y: auto;
      background-color: #f9f9f9;
    }
    .message {
      margin: 0.5rem 0;
    }
    .user {
      font-weight: bold;
    }
    .bot {
      color: #3333cc;
    }
    #input-area {
      display: flex;
      justify-content: center;
      max-width: 600px;
      margin: 0 auto;
    }
    #user-input {
      flex: 1;
      padding: 0.5rem;
    }
    #send-btn {
      padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
      margin-left: 0.5rem;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>BlenderBot Chat (facebook/blenderbot-400M-distill)</h1>

  <div id="chat-box"></div>

  <div id="input-area">
    <input type="text" id="user-input" placeholder="Type something..." />
    <button id="send-btn">Send</button>
  </div>

  <script>
    // Immediately invoked async function to let us use await
    (async () => {
      const { pipeline } = window.transformers;

      // 1. Load the text2text-generation pipeline for BlenderBot:
      const generator = await pipeline(
        "text2text-generation",
        "facebook/blenderbot-400M-distill"
      );

      // We'll store the entire conversation as text so we can pass it to the model each time.
      // A basic approach: prefix user messages with "User:" and BlenderBot with "Bot:".
      // For a better experience, you might refine prompts or use special tokens.
      let conversationHistory = "";

      const chatBox = document.getElementById("chat-box");
      const userInput = document.getElementById("user-input");
      const sendBtn = document.getElementById("send-btn");

      // Add a new message line to the chat box
      function addMessage(sender, text) {
        const msgDiv = document.createElement("div");
        msgDiv.classList.add("message", sender);
        msgDiv.textContent = sender === "user" 
          ? `User: ${text}` 
          : `Bot: ${text}`;
        chatBox.appendChild(msgDiv);
        chatBox.scrollTop = chatBox.scrollHeight;
      }

      // Handle sending a user message
      async function sendMessage() {
        const text = userInput.value.trim();
        if (!text) return;

        // Show user message in the chat
        addMessage("user", text);
        userInput.value = "";

        // Update conversation history
        conversationHistory += `User: ${text}\n`;

        // Prepare the prompt for BlenderBot
        // The model expects conversation-like input, so we feed it the entire history.
        const prompt = conversationHistory + "Bot:";

        try {
          // Generate response (the result of text2text-generation is an array of objects)
          const output = await generator(prompt, {
            max_new_tokens: 60,
          });
          const botReply = output && output[0] && output[0].generated_text
            ? output[0].generated_text
            : "(No response)";

          // Because BlenderBot might echo the conversation, let's parse just the last line
          // (Adjust this logic as needed based on model output).
          const cleanedReply = botReply.replace(prompt, "").trim();

          // Add Bot's message to conversation + chat
          conversationHistory += `Bot: ${cleanedReply}\n`;
          addMessage("bot", cleanedReply);
        } catch (err) {
          console.error("Generation error:", err);
          addMessage("bot", "Sorry, something went wrong!");
        }
      }

      // When user clicks "Send" or presses Enter
      sendBtn.addEventListener("click", sendMessage);
      userInput.addEventListener("keyup", (e) => {
        if (e.key === "Enter") {
          sendMessage();
        }
      });
    })();
  </script>
</body>
</html>

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

2) Serving the page

Because the model is large and Transformers.js may rely on web workers or fetch requests, make sure to serve your HTML from a simple local server (not file://). For example, in your project directory, run something like:

• Python 3.x:  
  python -m http.server 8080  
Then open http://localhost:8080 in your browser.

• Node.js (install serve globally):  
  npm install --global serve  
  serve .  

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

3) Conceptual flow

1. We load Transformers.js from a CDN.  
2. Use pipeline("text2text-generation", "facebook/blenderbot-400M-distill") to load the BlenderBot model.  
3. Keep a conversationHistory string containing all exchanges.  
4. On each user query:  
   – Append (“User: YOUR_MESSAGE”) to conversationHistory.  
   – Call the generator with prompt = conversationHistory + "Bot:".  
   – Parse the generated_text to extract Bot’s reply.  
   – Append (“Bot: REPLY”) to conversationHistory.  
5. Display both user input and bot reply in a simple chat interface.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

4) Next steps

• Fine-tuning the prompt: BlenderBot sometimes includes repeated text or partial context from the conversation. You can refine how you parse conversationHistory or strip repeated lines from the model output.  
• Add extra UI features: timestamps, nickname handling, etc.  
• Explore smaller or quantized models: Running large models fully in-browser can be slow or memory-intensive. Look for optimized or quantized variants if performance is an issue.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

That’s it! You now have a minimal BlenderBot chat app in pure vanilla JavaScript using Transformers.js. Make sure to watch the console for any load or memory warnings, since large LLMs can be quite heavy for client-side inference. If you need more advanced features (like better memory handling or skill switching), consider an approach with a backend server or more advanced pipeline usage.

Below is one example of how you might build a radial “slider” that lets users pick a circle radius, with concentric rings as visual markers. The basic idea is:

• Create a container that holds the concentric circles (the “slider”).  
• Place a draggable “handle” that the user can move closer or farther from the center.  
• Use JavaScript to track the mouse movement and update the radius based on the handle’s distance from center.  
• Provide concentric circles to help users gauge the selected radius.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1) HTML
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

<div id="radial-slider">
  <!-- Concentric circle markers -->
  <div class="circle-marker circle-size-1"></div>
  <div class="circle-marker circle-size-2"></div>
  <div class="circle-marker circle-size-3"></div>
  <div class="circle-marker circle-size-4"></div>

  <!-- The draggable handle -->
  <div id="handle"></div>

  <!-- Display the current radius -->
  <div id="radius-display">Radius: 0</div>
</div>

Explanation:  
• #radial-slider is the main container.  
• circle-marker elements are the concentric circles. Each one is sized differently via CSS.  
• #handle is a small circle that can be dragged around the center.  
• #radius-display shows the current radius value as text.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2) CSS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/* The wrapper that holds everything */
#radial-slider {
  position: relative;
  width: 300px;
  height: 300px;
  margin: 2rem auto;
  border: 2px dashed #ccc;
  border-radius: 50%;
  overflow: visible;
}

/* Concentric markers (just circles with rings) */
.circle-marker {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%; left: 50%;
  border: 2px solid #ccc;
  border-radius: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  pointer-events: none; /* Not clickable, allows clicks to pass through */
}

/* Example sizes for the markers */
.circle-size-1 { width: 100px; height: 100px; }
.circle-size-2 { width: 150px; height: 150px; }
.circle-size-3 { width: 200px; height: 200px; }
.circle-size-4 { width: 250px; height: 250px; }

/* Draggable handle */
#handle {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%; left: 50%;
  width: 20px; height: 20px;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: #ff5599;
  cursor: grab;
}

/* Radius display text */
#radius-display {
  position: absolute;
  top: 80%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translateX(-50%);
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.7);
  padding: 0.3rem 0.6rem;
  border-radius: 4px;
  font-family: sans-serif;
  pointer-events: none;
}

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3) JavaScript (to make the handle draggable radially)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

<script>
const slider     = document.getElementById("radial-slider");
const handle     = document.getElementById("handle");
const radiusDisp = document.getElementById("radius-display");

// For convenience, get the center of the container (in browser coordinates).
// We'll assume the container is not resized dynamically.
let sliderRect = slider.getBoundingClientRect();
let centerX    = sliderRect.left + sliderRect.width / 2;
let centerY    = sliderRect.top + sliderRect.height / 2;

// The maximum radius we'll allow, matching the largest circle-marker (~125 px from center)
const MAX_RADIUS = 125; 

// Track dragging state
let isDragging = false;

handle.addEventListener("mousedown", (e) => {
  isDragging = true;
  handle.style.cursor = "grabbing";
});

document.addEventListener("mouseup", (e) => {
  isDragging = false;
  handle.style.cursor = "grab";
});

document.addEventListener("mousemove", (e) => {
  if (!isDragging) return;

  // Mouse position
  const mouseX = e.clientX;
  const mouseY = e.clientY;

  // Distance from center
  const dx     = mouseX - centerX;
  const dy     = mouseY - centerY;
  let distance = Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);

  // Constrain to maximum
  if (distance > MAX_RADIUS) {
    distance = MAX_RADIUS;
  }

  // Calculate angle to maintain direction
  const angle = Math.atan2(dy, dx);

  // Compute new handle position (x, y) based on distance and angle
  const newX  = centerX + distance * Math.cos(angle);
  const newY  = centerY + distance * Math.sin(angle);

  // Move the handle element
  handle.style.left = newX + "px";
  handle.style.top  = newY + "px";

  // Update the text display (rounded to a neat integer)
  radiusDisp.textContent = "Radius: " + Math.round(distance);
});

// If the container might move on the page (e.g., due to window resizing),
// recalculate center positions on resize or scroll.
window.addEventListener("resize", () => {
  sliderRect = slider.getBoundingClientRect();
  centerX    = sliderRect.left + sliderRect.width / 2;
  centerY    = sliderRect.top + sliderRect.height / 2;
});
</script>

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
How this works
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• The concentric .circle-marker elements are positioned at the container’s center and differ in width/height. This gives the user visual reference points for the circle size.  
• The handle (#handle) starts in the center (translate(-50%, -50%)).  
• On mousedown, a flag (isDragging) is set to true, and we listen for mousemove events on the document.  
• Every time the mouse moves, we compute the distance (radius) from the center to the mouse location and clamp it so it doesn’t exceed MAX_RADIUS.  
• We also compute the angle (via atan2) so the handle moves in a straight line away from center, matching the user’s mouse direction.  
• The #radius-display element is updated with the current radius.  

This basic example can be expanded or styled further—adding more markers, smoothing the drag, using pointer events if supporting mobile, etc. But the core logic remains: find distance + angle from the center, move the handle, and update whatever radius you need in your application.

Below is an example of how you might implement a class "Circle" that inherits from a generic "Shape" base class. The "Shape" class here provides a template for computing area and perimeter, and raises NotImplementedError if those methods are not overridden by subclasses.

---------------------------------------------------------------
class Shape:
    def area(self):
        """Calculate the area of the shape."""
        raise NotImplementedError("Subclasses must implement this method.")

    def perimeter(self):
        """Calculate the perimeter of the shape."""
        raise NotImplementedError("Subclasses must implement this method.")

class Circle(Shape):
    def __init__(self, radius):
        self.radius = radius

    def area(self):
        import math
        return math.pi * (self.radius ** 2)

    def perimeter(self):
        import math
        return 2 * math.pi * self.radius

# Example usage:
# circle = Circle(radius=5)
# print(circle.area())      # Output: 78.53981633974483 (approximately)
# print(circle.perimeter()) # Output: 31.41592653589793 (approximately)
---------------------------------------------------------------

In this code:

• The Shape class defines the methods area() and perimeter() but does not implement them. Instead, it raises NotImplementedError to indicate that any subclass must override and provide its own implementation.
• The Circle class inherits from Shape and provides concrete implementations for area() and perimeter().

