Below is a minimal C example demonstrating how to handle a blocking read that may be interrupted by a signal (yielding EINTR). The essence is to retry the read call in a loop if it fails with errno set to EINTR.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/*
 * Example: Using a blocking read that can be interrupted by a signal.
 * The code installs a simple signal handler for SIGINT and demonstrates
 * how to properly handle EINTR by retrying the read().
 */

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <errno.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <string.h>

/* Global flag to show that we caught SIGINT */
static volatile sig_atomic_t got_sigint = 0;

/* Simple handler; set a flag when SIGINT is caught */
void sigint_handler(int signo) {
    (void)signo;  /* Unused in this handler */
    got_sigint = 1;
}

int main(void) {
    char buffer[128];
    ssize_t bytes_read;

    /* Install the signal handler for SIGINT (Ctrl-C) */
    struct sigaction sa;
    memset(&sa, 0, sizeof(sa));
    sa.sa_handler = sigint_handler;
    sigaction(SIGINT, &sa, NULL);

    printf("Type something and press Enter (press Ctrl-C to send SIGINT):\n");

    while (1) {
        bytes_read = read(STDIN_FILENO, buffer, sizeof(buffer) - 1);
        if (bytes_read == -1) {
            if (errno == EINTR) {
                /*
                 * read() was interrupted by a signal. If you want to continue 
                 * reading after the signal, just continue the loop.
                 */
                fprintf(stderr, "read() interrupted by signal, retrying...\n");

                /* If needed, you could also decide to stop if a signal was caught:
                   if (got_sigint) {
                       fprintf(stderr, "SIGINT caught. Exiting...\n");
                       break;
                   }
                */
                continue;
            } else {
                perror("read");
                exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
            }
        } else if (bytes_read == 0) {
            /* EOF or no more data */
            printf("EOF reached or no more data.\n");
            break;
        } else {
            /* Null-terminate the string and print */
            buffer[bytes_read] = '\0';
            printf("Read: %s", buffer);
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

How it works:
1. We set up a signal handler (sigint_handler) to catch SIGINT. This handler sets a global flag (got_sigint) when Ctrl-C is pressed.  
2. The main loop calls read() on STDIN_FILENO.  
3. If read() returns -1 and errno is EINTR, it means the call was interrupted by a signal. We handle it by simply continuing the loop (retrying the read).  
4. If read() returns 0, that indicates EOF (or end of available data).  
5. Otherwise, the function read() has successfully read some bytes, so we process them.  

This pattern—repeating a system call after EINTR—is essential for robust programs that must handle signals while using blocking I/O.

Below is a simple example of a C++ program that listens on port 888 (TCP), accepts an incoming connection, and reads data in a loop until the client disconnects or an error occurs. Note that this is a basic example demonstrating how to bind, listen, accept, and receive data. In a production environment, you would typically add more robust error handling, possibly use non-blocking I/O, and handle multiple connections.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// server.cpp
// A simple TCP server that listens on port 888 and reads incoming data.

#include <iostream>
#include <cstring>       // For memset
#include <cstdlib>       // For exit, EXIT_FAILURE
#include <netinet/in.h>  // For sockaddr_in
#include <unistd.h>      // For close

int main() {
    // Create a socket (IPv4, TCP)
    int server_fd = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
    if (server_fd < 0) {
        std::cerr << "Error: Failed to create socket.\n";
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    // Allow address reuse (optional but often useful in development)
    int opt_val = 1;
    if (setsockopt(server_fd, SOL_SOCKET, SO_REUSEADDR, &opt_val, sizeof(opt_val)) < 0) {
        std::cerr << "Error: setsockopt(SO_REUSEADDR) failed.\n";
        close(server_fd);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    // Bind the socket to port 888
    sockaddr_in server_addr;
    std::memset(&server_addr, 0, sizeof(server_addr));
    server_addr.sin_family = AF_INET;
    server_addr.sin_addr.s_addr = INADDR_ANY;   // Listen on any network interface
    server_addr.sin_port = htons(888);          // Host-to-network byte order for port

    if (bind(server_fd, reinterpret_cast<sockaddr*>(&server_addr), sizeof(server_addr)) < 0) {
        std::cerr << "Error: Failed to bind socket to port 888.\n";
        close(server_fd);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    // Start listening (backlog: 5)
    if (listen(server_fd, 5) < 0) {
        std::cerr << "Error: listen() failed.\n";
        close(server_fd);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    std::cout << "Server is listening on port 888...\n";

    // Accept a single incoming connection
    sockaddr_in client_addr;
    socklen_t client_len = sizeof(client_addr);
    int client_fd = accept(server_fd, reinterpret_cast<sockaddr*>(&client_addr), &client_len);

    if (client_fd < 0) {
        std::cerr << "Error: accept() failed.\n";
        close(server_fd);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    std::cout << "Client connected. Ready to read data.\n";

    // Read data in a loop
    const size_t BUFFER_SIZE = 1024;
    char buffer[BUFFER_SIZE];
    ssize_t bytes_received;

    while (true) {
        // Zero out the buffer
        std::memset(buffer, 0, BUFFER_SIZE);

        // Receive data
        bytes_received = recv(client_fd, buffer, BUFFER_SIZE - 1, 0);
        if (bytes_received < 0) {
            std::cerr << "Error: recv() failed.\n";
            break;
        }

        // If zero bytes were received, the client likely disconnected
        if (bytes_received == 0) {
            std::cout << "Client disconnected.\n";
            break;
        }

        // Print out the data received
        std::cout << "Received [" << bytes_received << " bytes]: " << buffer << "\n";
    }

    // Clean up
    close(client_fd);
    close(server_fd);
    return 0;
}
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

How to Compile & Run
1. Save this code to a file, for example server.cpp.
2. Compile using g++ or clang++:
   g++ -o server server.cpp
3. Run the resulting executable:
   ./server
4. Your program will listen on port 888. You can connect to it (for example, using telnet or netcat):
   nc 127.0.0.1 888
   and send text to see it appear on the server side. The server will print out whatever it receives.

This example only demonstrates handling one client at a time. For multiple connections, you’d typically either:
• Use multiple threads (one per client).  
• Use I/O multiplexing (select, poll, or epoll).  
For a production use-case, consider also adding proper error handling and logging.

Below is a sample C# function demonstrating how you might merge two Word documents using the OpenXML SDK, where you:

• Take from the first document:  
  – Exactly the first two “pages” of content (as best as WordprocessingML can delineate pages),  
  – Headers and footers,  
  – Overall design (styles, fonts, theme).  

• Take from the second document:  
  – Only the body contents (beyond page structure),  
  – The glossary (if present).  

• Include images embedded in both documents.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
IMPORTANT NOTES & LIMITATIONS
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

1. Word documents do not store an explicit “page” concept at the XML level. Page boundaries are determined dynamically by the layout engine. As a result, to approximate “first two pages,” one common approach is to look for explicit page break elements (e.g. <w:br w:type="page"/>), section breaks, or the <w:lastRenderedPageBreak> artifacts that may be generated by Word. This approach will not be 100% reliable in all cases; for example, if the source document doesn’t have manual page breaks or if lastRenderedPageBreak tags are missing or outdated.

2. Merging Word documents (especially partial merges) is non-trivial: aside from the body text, you may need to reconcile numbering definitions, relationships for images, style IDs, bookmarks, footnotes/endnotes, and more.

3. This example code is intended as a starting template. You may need additional logic for complex documents (e.g. advanced references, fields, tracked changes, custom XML parts, etc.).

4. If both documents contain a GlossaryDocumentPart, you may need to merge or rename conflicts in styles, images, or other references, rather than simply copying.

Below is a simplified illustration showing how you can copy relevant parts. Adjust or expand for your specific scenarios.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
EXAMPLE CODE
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

using DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Packaging;
using DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Wordprocessing;
using System.Linq;

public static class WordMerger
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Merges two Word documents using OpenXML:
    ///  - From the first document: first 2 pages, headers, footers, styles, and theme.
    ///  - From the second document: only the body text (all pages) and glossary.
    ///  - Preserves images from both.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="firstDocPath">Path to the first .docx file</param>
    /// <param name="secondDocPath">Path to the second .docx file</param>
    /// <param name="outputPath">Path to the merged output .docx</param>
    public static void MergeDocuments(
        string firstDocPath, 
        string secondDocPath, 
        string outputPath)
    {
        // 1. Create a new output document
        using (WordprocessingDocument outputDoc = 
               WordprocessingDocument.Create(outputPath, 
                                             DocumentFormat.OpenXml.WordprocessingDocumentType.Document))
        {
            // Add a MainDocumentPart to the output
            MainDocumentPart outputMainPart = outputDoc.AddMainDocumentPart();
            outputMainPart.Document = new Document(new Body());

            // 2. Open the source documents (read-only)
            using (WordprocessingDocument firstDoc = 
                   WordprocessingDocument.Open(firstDocPath, false))
            using (WordprocessingDocument secondDoc = 
                   WordprocessingDocument.Open(secondDocPath, false))
            {
                // 3. Copy overall design (styles, fonts, numbering, theme) from first doc
                CopyDocumentSettingsAndStyles(firstDoc, outputDoc);

                // 4. Copy headers and footers from the first doc
                CopyHeadersAndFooters(firstDoc, outputDoc);

                // 5. Get approximate first 2 pages from the first doc
                CopyFirstTwoPagesContent(firstDoc, outputDoc);

                // 6. Append the body content from the second doc
                CopyBodyContents(secondDoc, outputDoc);

                // 7. Copy Glossary part (if any) from the second doc
                CopyGlossaryPart(secondDoc, outputDoc);
            }

            // Finalize the main document
            outputMainPart.Document.Save();
        }
    }

    private static void CopyDocumentSettingsAndStyles(
        WordprocessingDocument sourceDoc, 
        WordprocessingDocument targetDoc)
    {
        // Copy DocumentSettingsPart
        DocumentSettingsPart sourceSettingsPart = sourceDoc.MainDocumentPart.DocumentSettingsPart;
        if (sourceSettingsPart != null)
        {
            var targetSettingsPart = targetDoc.MainDocumentPart.AddNewPart<DocumentSettingsPart>();
            targetSettingsPart.FeedData(sourceSettingsPart.GetStream());
        }

        // Copy StylesPart
        StylesPart sourceStylesPart = sourceDoc.MainDocumentPart.StyleDefinitionsPart;
        if (sourceStylesPart != null)
        {
            var targetStylesPart = targetDoc.MainDocumentPart.AddNewPart<StylesPart>();
            targetStylesPart.FeedData(sourceStylesPart.GetStream());
        }

        // Copy FontTablePart
        FontTablePart sourceFontPart = sourceDoc.MainDocumentPart.FontTablePart;
        if (sourceFontPart != null)
        {
            var targetFontPart = targetDoc.MainDocumentPart.AddNewPart<FontTablePart>();
            targetFontPart.FeedData(sourceFontPart.GetStream());
        }

        // Copy NumberingDefinitionsPart
        NumberingDefinitionsPart sourceNumberingPart = sourceDoc.MainDocumentPart.NumberingDefinitionsPart;
        if (sourceNumberingPart != null)
        {
            var targetNumberingPart = targetDoc.MainDocumentPart.AddNewPart<NumberingDefinitionsPart>();
            targetNumberingPart.FeedData(sourceNumberingPart.GetStream());
        }

        // Copy ThemePart
        ThemePart sourceThemePart = sourceDoc.MainDocumentPart.ThemePart;
        if (sourceThemePart != null)
        {
            var targetThemePart = targetDoc.MainDocumentPart.AddNewPart<ThemePart>();
            targetThemePart.FeedData(sourceThemePart.GetStream());
        }
    }

    private static void CopyHeadersAndFooters(
        WordprocessingDocument sourceDoc, 
        WordprocessingDocument targetDoc)
    {
        MainDocumentPart sourceMainPart = sourceDoc.MainDocumentPart;
        MainDocumentPart targetMainPart = targetDoc.MainDocumentPart;

        // Copy each HeaderPart
        foreach (var headerRel in sourceMainPart.HeaderParts)
        {
            var targetHeaderPart = targetMainPart.AddNewPart<HeaderPart>();
            targetHeaderPart.FeedData(headerRel.GetStream());

            // Re-establish relationship in the document
            var relativeId = sourceMainPart.GetIdOfPart(headerRel);
            targetMainPart.CreateRelationshipToPart(targetHeaderPart, relativeId);
        }

        // Copy each FooterPart
        foreach (var footerRel in sourceMainPart.FooterParts)
        {
            var targetFooterPart = targetMainPart.AddNewPart<FooterPart>();
            targetFooterPart.FeedData(footerRel.GetStream());

            // Re-establish relationship in the document
            var relativeId = sourceMainPart.GetIdOfPart(footerRel);
            targetMainPart.CreateRelationshipToPart(targetFooterPart, relativeId);
        }
    }

    private static void CopyFirstTwoPagesContent(
        WordprocessingDocument sourceDoc, 
        WordprocessingDocument targetDoc)
    {
        Body sourceBody = sourceDoc.MainDocumentPart.Document.Body;
        Body targetBody = targetDoc.MainDocumentPart.Document.Body;

        // We’ll look for explicit page breaks or <w:lastRenderedPageBreak>.
        // After encountering two page breaks, we stop copying.
        int pageCount = 0;

        foreach (var element in sourceBody.Elements())
        {
            // If we see a page break or lastRenderedPageBreak inside a paragraph/run, check it
            bool foundPageBreak = false;

            // Check if this element (often a Paragraph) contains a forced page break
            var paragraph = element as Paragraph;
            if (paragraph != null)
            {
                // Look for run breaks
                var breaks = paragraph.Descendants<Break>()
                                      .Where(b => b.Type != null && b.Type == BreakValues.Page);
                // or look for lastRenderedPageBreak
                var lastRendered = paragraph.Descendants<LastRenderedPageBreak>();

                foundPageBreak = breaks.Any() || lastRendered.Any();
            }

            // Add the element to the new doc
            targetBody.Append(element.CloneNode(true));

            if (foundPageBreak)
            {
                pageCount++;
                // Once we've captured two page breaks (thus ~2 pages), stop
                if (pageCount >= 2)
                {
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    private static void CopyBodyContents(
        WordprocessingDocument sourceDoc, 
        WordprocessingDocument targetDoc)
    {
        Body sourceBody = sourceDoc.MainDocumentPart.Document.Body;
        Body targetBody = targetDoc.MainDocumentPart.Document.Body;

        // Simply append all paragraphs/tables/etc. from the second doc
        foreach (var element in sourceBody.Elements())
        {
            targetBody.Append(element.CloneNode(true));
        }

        // Also ensure images/relationships from the second doc are copied
        // For each image relationship, replicate in target.  
        // A straightforward approach is to copy entire parts (like the below example).
        foreach (var part in sourceDoc.MainDocumentPart.Parts)
        {
            // If that part is an image part (or other relevant media), copy it
            var imagePart = part.OpenXmlPart as ImagePart;
            if (imagePart != null)
            {
                var targetImagePart = targetDoc.MainDocumentPart.AddPart(imagePart);
            }
        }
    }

    private static void CopyGlossaryPart(
        WordprocessingDocument sourceDoc, 
        WordprocessingDocument targetDoc)
    {
        // If the second doc has a GlossaryDocumentPart, copy it over
        GlossaryDocumentPart sourceGlossaryPart = sourceDoc.MainDocumentPart.GlossaryDocumentPart;
        if (sourceGlossaryPart != null)
        {
            var targetGlossaryPart = targetDoc.MainDocumentPart.AddNewPart<GlossaryDocumentPart>();
            targetGlossaryPart.FeedData(sourceGlossaryPart.GetStream());

            // Copy images or other resources inside the glossary as needed
            foreach (var part in sourceGlossaryPart.Parts)
            {
                if (part.OpenXmlPart is ImagePart sourceImagePart)
                {
                    var targetImagePart = targetGlossaryPart.AddPart(sourceImagePart);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
USAGE EXAMPLE
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/*
class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        string firstDoc = @"C:\Docs\FirstDocument.docx";
        string secondDoc = @"C:\Docs\SecondDocument.docx";
        string outputDoc = @"C:\Docs\MergedDocument.docx";

        WordMerger.MergeDocuments(firstDoc, secondDoc, outputDoc);

        Console.WriteLine("Documents merged successfully!");
    }
}
*/

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

HOW IT WORKS (HIGH-LEVEL):

1) Create a new output Word document (MergedDocument.docx).  
2) From the first document:  
   • Copy design (styles, fonts, numbering, theme) so the new document looks the same.  
   • Copy headers and footers so they appear in the merged file.  
   • Extract up to 2 pages of content by searching for page-break markers (this is an approximation).  
3) From the second document:  
   • Append the body contents to continue after the first document’s content.  
   • Copy the GlossaryDocumentPart (if present), which holds building blocks such as cover pages or custom galleries.  
4) Write the merged result to disk.  

Because Word does not natively store “page 1,” “page 2,” etc. in a simple way, this example relies on scanning for explicit page breaks. In real-world documents, you may need more robust logic or rely on consistent use of page/section breaks within the source files.  

This code should serve as a starting point. You can refine and expand it depending on how your documents are structured and how reliably they use page or section breaks.

