Below is a simple example of a Laravel Middleware that checks the incoming request IP against an array of allowed IP addresses. If the IP is not in the list, the middleware aborts with a 403 response.

Step 1: Create the middleware.  
Run the following Artisan command (or create the file manually) to generate a middleware class:  
php artisan make:middleware IpWhitelist  

Step 2: Open the generated middleware (app/Http/Middleware/IpWhitelist.php) and add your whitelist logic:

-----------------------------------
<?php

namespace App\Http\Middleware;

use Closure;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;

class IpWhitelist
{
    /**
     * Handle an incoming request.
     */
    public function handle(Request $request, Closure $next)
    {
        // Define allowed IP addresses here
        $allowedIps = [
            '127.0.0.1',
            '192.168.0.5',
            // Add more as needed
        ];

        // Check if the requesting IP is allowed
        if (!in_array($request->ip(), $allowedIps, true)) {
            // Block the user with a 403
            abort(403, 'Your IP is not allowed to access this resource.');
        }

        return $next($request);
    }
}
-----------------------------------

Step 3: Register the middleware.  
In app/Http/Kernel.php, find the $routeMiddleware array and add your new middleware:

protected $routeMiddleware = [
    // ...
    'ip.whitelist' => \App\Http\Middleware\IpWhitelist::class,
];

Step 4: Apply the middleware to routes or controllers.  
You can protect specific routes by attaching the middleware in your routes file (routes/web.php or routes/api.php, etc.):

Route::middleware(['ip.whitelist'])->group(function () {
    Route::get('/dashboard', [DashboardController::class, 'index']);
    // Add more protected routes here
});

Or apply it to a single route:

Route::get('/dashboard', [DashboardController::class, 'index'])->middleware('ip.whitelist');

With these steps, any request that is not from one of the allowed IPs will receive a 403 Forbidden response, effectively blocking them from accessing your protected routes.

