In most cases, “modCount” is used only for fail‐fast behavior (like in the JDK’s collections) and not for real concurrency support. Hence, the simplest fix is to remove the volatile keyword and just use a plain int, since incrementing a volatile int is still not an atomic operation in Java.

If you really need atomic increments (for true thread-safety), then replace the field with an AtomicInteger and use the incrementAndGet() method. For instance:

private final AtomicInteger modCount = new AtomicInteger(0);

Then each time you need to increment:

modCount.incrementAndGet();

And when checking:

if (modCount.get() != expectedModCount) {
    throw new ConcurrentModificationException();
}

That eliminates the warning and ensures genuinely atomic increments should you require concurrency. However, if you’re only mimicking the usual “fail‐fast” pattern (as in ArrayList, etc.) and you do not support multi-threaded use, simply removing volatile and using an int is the typical approach.

Below is one common way to handle a C-style struct with a fixed‐size array in HighFive: you specialize HighFive’s create_compound_type for your Data struct and then insert each member (including the array) into the compound type.

For your struct:

  struct Data {
      double v[3];
      int n;
  };

you can do something like this:

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Include HighFive headers
#include <highfive/H5DataSet.hpp>
#include <highfive/H5File.hpp>
#include <highfive/H5DataSpace.hpp>
#include <highfive/H5CompoundType.hpp>
#include <iostream>

// Your struct
struct Data {
    double v[3];
    int n;
};

// Specialize create_compound_type for your struct
namespace HighFive {

template <>
inline CompoundType create_compound_type<Data>() {
    // Create a CompoundType of the total size of Data
    CompoundType compound(sizeof(Data));

    // Insert the array “v” (3 doubles)
    compound.insertMember(
        "v",                                 // Name of field in HDF5
        HOFFSET(Data, v),                    // Byte offset inside Data
        AtomicType<double>(),                // Atomic HDF5 type for doubles
        3                                    // Number of array elements
    );

    // Insert the int “n”
    compound.insertMember(
        "n", 
        HOFFSET(Data, n), 
        AtomicType<int>()
    );

    return compound;
}

} // namespace HighFive
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

How it works:

• You define a free function template <>, “create_compound_type<Data>()”, that returns a HighFive::CompoundType describing how your struct’s memory layout maps onto HDF5 fields.  
• compound.insertMember(...) lets you add each field to the compound datatype.  
  – For array fields (e.g., v[3]) you supply the number of elements as the last argument.  
  – For scalar fields (e.g., n) you do not specify that last argument.  
• HOFFSET(Data, fieldName) comes from the HDF5 C API; it computes the byte offset of the named field in your struct.  

Once you have this specialization, you can straightforwardly write and read Data objects as a compound type:

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Example usage:
int main() {
    // Create an HDF5 file
    HighFive::File file("test.h5", HighFive::File::Overwrite);

    // Prepare some data to write
    std::vector<Data> dataVec(2);
    dataVec[0].v[0] =  1.1; dataVec[0].v[1] = 2.2; dataVec[0].v[2] = 3.3;
    dataVec[0].n = 42;
    dataVec[1].v[0] = -4.4; dataVec[1].v[1] = 5.5; dataVec[1].v[2] = 6.6;
    dataVec[1].n = 99;

    // Create a dataset and write
    HighFive::DataSet dataset =
        file.createDataSet<Data>("my_data", HighFive::DataSpace::From(dataVec));
    dataset.write(dataVec);

    // Read back
    std::vector<Data> readVec;
    dataset.read(readVec);

    // Print results
    for (auto const& d : readVec) {
        std::cout << "v[] = {" << d.v[0] << ", " << d.v[1] << ", " << d.v[2]
                  << "}, n = " << d.n << "\n";
    }

    return 0;
}
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

That’s all you need to treat your struct with a fixed‐size array as a compound type in HighFive.

Below is a common pattern for providing a “default” mock in a Jest setup file (e.g., via setupFilesAfterEnv) and then overriding that mock in an individual test when needed. The key idea is to do a global mock in the setup file and then tweak or override that mock in your test file.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
1) Configure your Jest setup file in jest.config.js
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
For example, in jest.config.js or in your package.json jest section:

  // jest.config.js
  module.exports = {
    // ...other config
    setupFilesAfterEnv: ["<rootDir>/jest.setup.js"],
  };

This will ensure that jest.setup.js runs before any test files are executed.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
2) Create jest.setup.js with a default mock
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
In this file, you provide a default mock implementation. Suppose we are mocking a module named myModule:

  // jest.setup.js
  jest.mock('myModule', () => {
    return {
      someMethod: jest.fn(() => 'default mock value'),
    };
  });

This means every test that runs after this file is loaded will see that myModule has someMethod returning 'default mock value'—unless you override it.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
3) Override the mock in your test
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
To use a different implementation of the same mocked module in one specific test suite or test, you can override the existing default mock. A common approach is to import your mocked module in your test, then directly change the mock implementation:

  // myModule.test.js
  import { someMethod } from 'myModule';

  describe('myModule tests', () => {
    test('uses the default mock from setupFilesAfterEnv', () => {
      expect(someMethod()).toBe('default mock value');
    });

    test('overrides the mock implementation in this test', () => {
      // Change the implementation on the fly
      someMethod.mockImplementation(() => 'overridden mock value');

      expect(someMethod()).toBe('overridden mock value');
    });
  });

This approach works well because:
• The global or “default” mock is set up before every test file.  
• In any particular test (or test suite), you can modify the mock functions (e.g., with mockImplementation, mockReturnValue, etc.) to customize behavior.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Key Points
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
• Because jest.mock(...) is hoisted to the top of the module, you typically declare those calls before importing the module you wish to mock in your test.  
• If all you need to do is slightly alter the behavior of a mocked function, you can simply change the implementation in your test (as shown above) rather than calling jest.mock(...) again.  
• If you need a brand new mock for an entire test file, you can also call jest.resetModules() or jest.mock('module', { … }) at the top of that file; just be mindful of hoisting rules and that it may overwrite your global mock.  

Using this pattern, you get both a convenient default mock (in setupFilesAfterEnv) and the flexibility to modify (or completely replace) that mock in any individual test file.

