Util.doSomething();
Util.doSomethingElse();
--------------------------------

This way, you can call both functions from the single default export (Util).

Yes, but not directly via the “classic” Node.js Stream interface. Node’s built‐in stream API is conceptually push‐based—under the hood, data is pushed into buffers as it becomes available. That said, there are at least two common approaches that effectively let you consume data in a pull‐like fashion:

1) Using the “readable” mode and manually calling .read():  
   • When using a Readable stream in “readable” mode, Node will emit the "readable" event whenever new data is available.  
   • You can then call stream.read([size]) in the "readable" handler to pull data out of the internal buffer at your own pace.  
   • Note: if you attach a "data" event handler, the stream switches to flowing mode, rendering the manual read() approach less directly useful.

   Example:

   const fs = require('fs');

   const readable = fs.createReadStream('file.txt', { encoding: 'utf8' });

   readable.on('readable', () => {
     let chunk;
     while ((chunk = readable.read()) !== null) {
       console.log('Got chunk:', chunk);
       // This read call is the "pull" - you decide when/if
       // to call .read() again.
     }
   });

   readable.on('end', () => {
     console.log('No more data.');
   });

2) Consuming Readable streams as async iterators:  
   • Node >= 10 supports async iteration over Readable streams. This again gives you a more pull-like interface.  
   • Each iteration of for await (const chunk of <stream>) pulls one chunk at a time.  

   Example:

   const fs = require('fs');

   (async () => {
     const readable = fs.createReadStream('file.txt', { encoding: 'utf8' });
     for await (const chunk of readable) {
       // Each iteration "pulls" the next chunk
       console.log('Got chunk:', chunk);
     }
     console.log('No more data.');
   })();

When you iterate in this style, control flow stays in your async function; each loop iteration waits for the “next” chunk to be available, effectively pulling data in a stepwise manner.

Alternative: Using pull-stream library  
If you need a truly pull-based paradigm (e.g., a functional style that never “pushes” automatically), you can look at the community-maintained pull-stream library. It was designed with an entirely pull-driven approach in mind, but note that it isn’t part of core Node.js.

In summary, while Node’s streams are natively more push-oriented, you can treat them in a pull-like way grâce to “readable” mode or async iteration. If you want a fully “pure” pull-oriented API, consider third-party libraries like pull-stream.

In JavaScript, .map() is only defined on arrays, not on plain objects. The error “TypeError: x.map is not a function” occurs because x is an object ({ "one": 1 }) rather than an array. To fix this, you can do one of the following:

1) Use an array instead of an object.  
   For example, if you really meant to have an array of values, you could write:

   let x = [1]; 
   // Now x is an array and we can call .map
   const result = x.map(z => z + 1); 
   console.log(result); // [2]

2) If you need to work with object properties, use Object methods (Object.keys, Object.values, or Object.entries).  
   For example, to increment the values of an object, you might do:

   let x = { one: 1 };
   const incremented = Object.entries(x).reduce((acc, [key, value]) => {
     acc[key] = value + 1;
     return acc;
   }, {});
   console.log(incremented); // { one: 2 }

3) If you want to extract the object values into an array, use Object.values, then map over the resulting array:

   let x = { one: 1 };
   const mappedValues = Object.values(x).map(val => val + 1);
   console.log(mappedValues); // [2]

Which choice you make depends on how you need to use x in your program. But the key point is that .map is not defined on plain objects—only on arrays (and array-like structures).

Below is a minimal example of a single-pass 5×5 Gaussian blur in a Metal compute kernel. It reads from an input texture and writes the blurred result to an output texture. This kernel uses a fixed 5×5 Gaussian kernel (normalized to sum to 1) and clamps sample coordinates at image edges. In practice, you would dispatch this compute kernel at least as large as your image resolution.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// GaussianBlur.metal
#include <metal_stdlib>
using namespace metal;

// Structures can hold parameters such as image dimensions.
struct Uniforms
{
    uint width;   // Image width
    uint height;  // Image height
};

kernel void gaussianBlur(
    texture2d<float, access::read>  inTexture    [[ texture(0) ]],
    texture2d<float, access::write> outTexture   [[ texture(1) ]],
    constant Uniforms&              uniforms     [[ buffer(0) ]],
    uint2                           gid          [[ thread_position_in_grid ]]
)
{
    // Ignore threads that fall outside the image dimensions.
    if (gid.x >= uniforms.width || gid.y >= uniforms.height)
        return;

    // A 5x5 Gaussian kernel with radius = 2, sum of weights = 256.
    constexpr float kernel[5][5] = {
        {1.0/256.0f,  4.0/256.0f,  6.0/256.0f,  4.0/256.0f,  1.0/256.0f},
        {4.0/256.0f, 16.0/256.0f, 24.0/256.0f, 16.0/256.0f,  4.0/256.0f},
        {6.0/256.0f, 24.0/256.0f, 36.0/256.0f, 24.0/256.0f,  6.0/256.0f},
        {4.0/256.0f, 16.0/256.0f, 24.0/256.0f, 16.0/256.0f,  4.0/256.0f},
        {1.0/256.0f,  4.0/256.0f,  6.0/256.0f,  4.0/256.0f,  1.0/256.0f}
    };

    float4 blurredColor = float4(0.0);

    // Sample each pixel in the 5x5 neighborhood around (gid.x, gid.y).
    for (int rowOffset = -2; rowOffset <= 2; rowOffset++)
    {
        for (int colOffset = -2; colOffset <= 2; colOffset++)
        {
            // Clamp coordinates so we don’t read outside the image.
            int2 sampleCoord = int2(gid) + int2(colOffset, rowOffset);
            sampleCoord.x = clamp(sampleCoord.x, 0, (int)uniforms.width  - 1);
            sampleCoord.y = clamp(sampleCoord.y, 0, (int)uniforms.height - 1);

            // Read the sample.
            float4 sampleColor = inTexture.read(uint2(sampleCoord));

            // Accumulate weighted color.
            blurredColor += sampleColor * kernel[rowOffset + 2][colOffset + 2];
        }
    }

    // Write the final blurred result.
    outTexture.write(blurredColor, gid);
}
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Explanation:
• A 5×5 Gaussian weights array is baked into the kernel with a radius of 2.  
• Each thread corresponds to one output pixel. The kernel sums samples from a 5×5 neighborhood and multiplies each sample by the appropriate weight from the Gaussian kernel.  
• Sample coordinates are clamped so that edge pixels don’t read outside the image.  
• This is a single-pass version. Sometimes, a two-pass (horizontal then vertical) approach is more performant for larger radii, but this code works well for small kernels.

Below is a high-level overview and example of how you might implement a basic SCIM (System for Cross-domain Identity Management) 2.0–compatible server in PHP using the Slim Framework as your router. 

▌1. Understand SCIM Basics
SCIM is a standard for managing identity data (e.g., users and groups) across domains or services. The SCIM 2.0 specification defines:
• A set of standard endpoints for managing Users, Groups, and other resource types.  
• A standardized JSON-based data format and schema.  
• Operations for create, retrieve, update (PUT/PATCH), and delete resources.  
• Query parameters for filtering and pagination.  

Key endpoints typically include:  
• /scim/v2/ServiceProviderConfig  
• /scim/v2/ResourceTypes  
• /scim/v2/Schemas  
• /scim/v2/Users  
• /scim/v2/Groups  

▌2. Set Up Your Project
1. Initialize a new Composer project and install Slim (v4).  
2. Additionally, install a PSR-7 and PSR-17 implementation (such as slim/psr7 or nyholm/psr7):  

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
composer init
composer require slim/slim:^4.0 slim/psr7
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Your composer.json would look something like:

{
  "require": {
    "slim/slim": "^4.0",
    "slim/psr7": "^1.6"
  }
}

▌3. Basic Directory Structure
A common structure might look like this:

my-scim-server/
├── composer.json
├── vendor/
└── public/
    └── index.php            // main front controller

▌4. Bootstrap Slim in index.php
Below is a minimal example of setting up Slim and handling SCIM routes. You can expand each route to meet the SCIM 2.0 spec requirements.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
<?php
// public/index.php

use Psr\Http\Message\ResponseInterface as Response;
use Psr\Http\Message\ServerRequestInterface as Request;
use Slim\Factory\AppFactory;
use Slim\Routing\RouteCollectorProxy;

require __DIR__ . '/../vendor/autoload.php';

$app = AppFactory::create();

// Optionally set a base path if you have a subfolder or reverse proxy
// $app->setBasePath('/my-scim-server');

// Group SCIM endpoints under /scim/v2
$app->group('/scim/v2', function (RouteCollectorProxy $group) {

    // SCIM /ServiceProviderConfig endpoint
    $group->get('/ServiceProviderConfig', function (Request $request, Response $response) {
        // Provide your SCIM service provider configuration
        // See https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7644#section-4
        $configData = [
            'schemas' => [
              'urn:ietf:params:scim:schemas:core:2.0:ServiceProviderConfig'
            ],
            'documentationUri' => 'https://example.com/scim/v2/documentation',
            'patch' => ['supported' => true],
            'bulk'  => ['supported' => false],
            'filter' => ['supported' => true],
            'changePassword' => ['supported' => false],
            'sort' => ['supported' => true],
            'etag' => ['supported' => false],
            'authenticationSchemes' => [
              [
                'type' => 'oauthbearertoken',
                'name' => 'OAuth Bearer Token',
                'description' => 'Authentication Scheme using the OAuth Bearer Token Standard',
                'specUri' => 'http://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc6750',
                'primary' => true
              ]
            ]
        ];
        $response->getBody()->write(json_encode($configData));
        return $response
            ->withHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json')
            ->withStatus(200);
    });

    // SCIM /ResourceTypes endpoint
    $group->get('/ResourceTypes', function (Request $request, Response $response) {
        // Provide SCIM ResourceTypes metadata if desired
        // Example includes only "User" and "Group"
        $resourceTypes = [
            [
                'id' => 'User',
                'name' => 'User',
                'endpoint' => '/scim/v2/Users',
                'description' => 'User Account'
            ],
            [
                'id' => 'Group',
                'name' => 'Group',
                'endpoint' => '/scim/v2/Groups',
                'description' => 'Group'
            ]
        ];
        $response->getBody()->write(json_encode($resourceTypes));
        return $response
            ->withHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json')
            ->withStatus(200);
    });

    // SCIM /Schemas endpoint
    // Typically you would return user, group, and custom schemas here
    $group->get('/Schemas', function (Request $request, Response $response) {
        $schemas = [
            // Provide the standard SCIM schema for User, Group, etc.
        ];
        $response->getBody()->write(json_encode($schemas));
        return $response
            ->withHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json')
            ->withStatus(200);
    });

    // SCIM /Users endpoint
    $group->get('/Users', function (Request $request, Response $response) {
        // Handle retrieving a list of users (possibly with filtering, pagination, etc.)
        // For demonstration, return a simple static list
        $users = [
            'totalResults' => 1,
            'Resources' => [
                [
                    'schemas'    => ['urn:ietf:params:scim:schemas:core:2.0:User'],
                    'id'         => '2819c223-7f76-453a-919d-413861904646',
                    'userName'   => 'bjensen',
                    'name'       => [
                        'givenName'  => 'Barbara',
                        'familyName' => 'Jensen'
                    ]
                ]
            ]
        ];
        $response->getBody()->write(json_encode($users));
        return $response->withHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
    });

    // Create a new user
    $group->post('/Users', function (Request $request, Response $response) {
        $body = json_decode($request->getBody()->getContents(), true);
        // Validate SCIM user data & insert into database
        // Return the newly created user resource with a 201 status
        $createdUser = [
            'schemas'    => ['urn:ietf:params:scim:schemas:core:2.0:User'],
            'id'         => 'generated-user-id-here',
            'userName'   => $body['userName'] ?? '',
            'name'       => $body['name'] ?? []
        ];
        $response->getBody()->write(json_encode($createdUser));
        return $response
            ->withHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json')
            ->withStatus(201);
    });

    // Retrieve a specific user
    $group->get('/Users/{id}', function (Request $request, Response $response, $args) {
        $userId = $args['id'];
        // Fetch user from database or data store
        // If not found, return 404, else return the user
        // Here is a mock example:
        $user = [
            'schemas'    => ['urn:ietf:params:scim:schemas:core:2.0:User'],
            'id'         => $userId,
            'userName'   => 'jdoe',
            'name'       => [
                'givenName'  => 'John',
                'familyName' => 'Doe'
            ]
        ];
        $response->getBody()->write(json_encode($user));
        return $response->withHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
    });

    // Replace a user with PUT
    $group->put('/Users/{id}', function (Request $request, Response $response, $args) {
        $userId = $args['id'];
        $body = json_decode($request->getBody()->getContents(), true);
        // Perform a full replacement of the user resource
        // Return updated user resource
        $updatedUser = [
            'schemas'    => ['urn:ietf:params:scim:schemas:core:2.0:User'],
            'id'         => $userId,
            'userName'   => $body['userName'] ?? '',
            'name'       => $body['name'] ?? []
        ];
        $response->getBody()->write(json_encode($updatedUser));
        return $response
            ->withHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json')
            ->withStatus(200);
    });

    // Modify a user with PATCH
    $group->patch('/Users/{id}', function (Request $request, Response $response, $args) {
        $userId = $args['id'];
        $body = json_decode($request->getBody()->getContents(), true);
        // Apply partial updates based on operations in "Operations" array
        // Example SCIM Patch request structure: {"Operations":[ ... ]}
        // Return updated user resource
        $patchedUser = [
            'schemas'  => ['urn:ietf:params:scim:schemas:core:2.0:User'],
            'id'       => $userId,
            // Merge changes here
        ];
        $response->getBody()->write(json_encode($patchedUser));
        return $response
            ->withHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json')
            ->withStatus(200);
    });

    // Delete a user
    $group->delete('/Users/{id}', function (Request $request, Response $response, array $args) {
        $userId = $args['id'];
        // Delete user from data store
        // Return empty response or success message
        return $response->withStatus(204);
    });

    // Similar endpoints for Groups could go here:
    // $group->get('/Groups', ...);
    // $group->post('/Groups', ...);
    // etc.
});

// Run the Slim app
$app->run();
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

▌5. Implement SCIM Logic & Data Storage
1. Authentication & Authorization:  
   • SCIM endpoints often require OAuth Bearer tokens, BasicAuth, or some other mechanism.  
   • Validate incoming tokens before performing SCIM operations.  

2. Data Storage:  
   • Store users/groups in a database, LDAP, or any data store.  
   • Map SCIM fields (e.g., userName, name.givenName, emails, etc.) to your internal data structure.  

3. SCIM Query Parameters (Filtering, Pagination, Sorting):  
   • The SCIM spec specifies attributes like startIndex, count, filter, sortBy, and sortOrder.  
   • You should parse these query params to return the correct subset of resources.  

4. Schema Validation:  
   • SCIM uses standard schemas for Users and Groups.  
   • In production, you should validate incoming payloads to ensure they match SCIM schemas.  

▌6. Testing
• Test the server endpoints using a REST client (e.g., curl, Postman) or a SCIM client library.  
• Verify that each endpoint is returning/accepting data in JSON format compliant with SCIM.  

▌7. Production Considerations
• Error Handling: Return SCIM-style error objects (RFC 7644).  
• Performance & Scalability: Efficiently handle large user directories and filtering.  
• Security: Ensure TLS (HTTPS) is used; authenticate all requests.  

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

In summary, creating a SCIM server in PHP using Slim involves:  
• Setting up the Slim application and grouping SCIM routes under /scim/v2.  
• Implementing GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, and DELETE for /Users and /Groups (and optionally other SCIM resource types).  
• Returning JSON responses according to the SCIM 2.0 spec (including correct status codes and schema structures).  
• Handling authentication, error responses, and SCIM query parameters.

You can expand upon this skeleton to implement the full SCIM specification, handle complex filtering, and integrate your own data store. This gives you a starting point to build a fully compliant SCIM service.

