#include "mnist_hls.h"

// Simple ReLU activation (example). You can also use sigmoid/tanh, etc.
static data_t relu(data_t x) {
    return (x > 0) ? x : (data_t)0;
}

// Top-level function to be compiled by Vitis HLS.
// input:  784 values (flattened 28x28 pixel intensities).
// pred_digit: Output is the predicted class [0..9].
void mnist_inference(const data_t input[INPUT_SIZE], int &pred_digit) {
#pragma HLS INTERFACE mode=s_axilite port=return        // Standard HLS interface directive
#pragma HLS INTERFACE mode=s_axilite port=input bundle=IN_DATA
#pragma HLS INTERFACE mode=s_axilite port=pred_digit
    // You can also consider other interfaces like AXI Streaming depending on your system.

    // Internal buffers for hidden layer and output
    data_t hidden[HIDDEN_SIZE];
    data_t output[OUTPUT_SIZE];

#pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=hidden  complete
#pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=output  complete

    // First layer: hidden = relu(W1 * input + B1)
    // ------------------------------------------------
    L1:
    for(int i = 0; i < HIDDEN_SIZE; i++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
        data_t acc = B1[i];
        W1_ACCUM:
        for(int j = 0; j < INPUT_SIZE; j++) {
            acc += W1[i][j] * input[j];
        }
        hidden[i] = relu(acc);
    }

    // Second layer: output = W2 * hidden + B2
    // (Optionally apply a softmax or just pick argmax)
    // ------------------------------------------------
    L2:
    for(int i = 0; i < OUTPUT_SIZE; i++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
        data_t acc = B2[i];
        W2_ACCUM:
        for(int j = 0; j < HIDDEN_SIZE; j++) {
            acc += W2[i][j] * hidden[j];
        }
        output[i] = acc; // Typically you could apply an activation or keep raw logits
    }

    // Argmax for final classification
    // ------------------------------------------------
    int max_index = 0;
    data_t max_val = output[0];
    ARGMAX_LOOP:
    for(int i = 1; i < OUTPUT_SIZE; i++) {
        if(output[i] > max_val) {
            max_val   = output[i];
            max_index = i;
        }
    }

    // Return the index of the maximum output
    pred_digit = max_index;
}

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
How to use and extend this code
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1) Provide trained weights and biases:
   • You must provide the actual values for W1, B1, W2, and B2 in mnist_hls.h based on your trained MNIST model.  
   • For a simple two-layer network, if you trained a standard model in Python (TensorFlow/PyTorch), just export the .weight and .bias parameters to text or header format, then copy them into the arrays.

2) Synthesize in Vitis HLS:
   • Create a new Vitis HLS project.  
   • Add mnist_hls.cpp and mnist_hls.h.  
   • Choose mnist_inference as the top-level function.  
   • Synthesize, implement optimizations (#pragma HLS for unrolling, pipelining, etc.), and export the RTL.

3) Adjust the network topology and data types:
   • For production or resource-optimized designs, change data_t to a suitable ap_fixed<…> type.  
   • Expand the hidden layer to a larger size (e.g., 128 or 256) for better accuracy.  
   • Add more layers (convolutional, deeper fully connected, etc.) if desired.

4) Handling the input image data:
   • The input is a flattened 28×28 array, typically normalized (e.g., 0 to 1).  
   • In real deployment, you might stream in these 784 pixels, store them in a small on-chip buffer, then call mnist_inference.

5) Post-processing the output:
   • Optionally you can apply a software-level softmax once you have the 10 raw logits in output[].  
   • Often you only need the argmax (the digit label).

This skeleton demonstrates the main structure of a basic hardware-friendly MNIST inference. You should adapt, optimize, and expand it according to your project’s performance (latency/throughput) and resource (LUTs/BRAM/DSP) goals.

