In Rust, a closure can be one of three “function-like” traits:

• FnOnce: consumes the variables it captures, so it can be called only once.  
• FnMut: may mutate captured variables, so it can be called multiple times, but not concurrently.  
• Fn: does not mutate or consume the captured variables, so it can be called any number of times.  

If you get a compiler error saying “expected Fn but got FnOnce,” that means the closure cannot be called more than once because it “moves” (consumes) something from its environment. A few common scenarios and fixes:

1) Remove the “move” keyword if not needed  
   Often, adding move to a closure forces it to capture variables by value instead of by reference. By removing move, you allow the compiler to capture by reference if that satisfies the lifetime requirements. This typically allows the closure to implement Fn or FnMut instead of FnOnce.  

   // BAD: closure is forced to capture ownership of variables
   let x = String::from("hello");
   let closure = move || println!("{}", x);
   // closure is FnOnce because it takes ownership of x

   // BETTER: remove `move` if you only need a reference
   let x = String::from("hello");
   let closure = || println!("{}", x);
   // closure is now Fn or FnMut (depending on usage) because it borrows x

2) Use references or shared ownership instead of moving ownership  
   If you do need partial ownership or a shared reference inside the closure, you can reference your data without fully moving it. Or if you really need shared ownership, use something like an Arc (for concurrency-safe sharing) or Rc (for single-threaded sharing).

   // Using references:
   let mut value = 10;
   let closure = || value += 1; // captured by mutable reference ⇒ FnMut
   // This is valid to call multiple times

3) Convert exclusive mutable captures to interior mutability when possible  
   If the closure mutates something captured from the environment but you need it to be callable multiple times, try interior mutability. For example, a RefCell, Mutex, or similar can let you modify data without taking full ownership in the closure.

   use std::cell::RefCell;
   let data = RefCell::new(10);
   let closure = || {
       *data.borrow_mut() += 1; // interior mutability
   };
   // This closure is Fn since it only borrows mutably via RefCell internally

Any time a closure takes ownership of a variable and is allowed to consume or “move out” of it, it can only be called once (FnOnce). To fix this, ensure your closure does not move out of its captures unless that is actually required.

Below is an example Rust function that takes a month name as a string and returns its corresponding month number (1 for January, 2 for February, etc.). If the input doesn’t match any known month, the function returns None.

-----------------------------------------------------
use std::fmt;

#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]
pub enum MonthError {
    InvalidMonthName,
}

impl fmt::Display for MonthError {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
        match self {
            MonthError::InvalidMonthName => write!(f, "Invalid month name"),
        }
    }
}

pub fn month_to_number(month: &str) -> Result<u8, MonthError> {
    match month.to_lowercase().as_str() {
        "january" => Ok(1),
        "february" => Ok(2),
        "march" => Ok(3),
        "april" => Ok(4),
        "may" => Ok(5),
        "june" => Ok(6),
        "july" => Ok(7),
        "august" => Ok(8),
        "september" => Ok(9),
        "october" => Ok(10),
        "november" => Ok(11),
        "december" => Ok(12),
        _ => Err(MonthError::InvalidMonthName),
    }
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn test_month_to_number() {
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("January"), Ok(1));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("FeBRuAry"), Ok(2));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("December"), Ok(12));
        assert!(month_to_number("NotAMonth").is_err());
    }
}

-----------------------------------------------------

Explanation:
• month_to_number takes a &str representing a month name. 
• The function normalizes the month to lowercase and matches it against known months. 
• If a match is found, it returns Ok with the corresponding month number. Otherwise, it returns an Err(MonthError::InvalidMonthName).
• In the test module, basic tests are provided to verify correctness.

