Yes. The short version is that the function save_data() is doing an out‐of‐bounds write when it uses .chars() (32‐bit Rust chars) and then writes them directly into a u8 buffer. Because each “Rust char” is four bytes, a user who supplies close to 200 characters can overrun the array on the stack and overwrite nearby data—potentially including login_pin or even the constant controlling the PIN check.

Below is a more detailed explanation:

1) How save_data() works:
   fn save_data(dest: &mut [u8], src: &String) {
       if src.chars().count() > INPUT_SIZE {
           println!("Oups, something went wrong...");
           std::process::exit(1);
       }

       let mut dest_ptr = dest.as_mut_ptr() as *mut char;

       unsafe {
           for c in src.chars() {
               dest_ptr.write(c);
               dest_ptr = dest_ptr.offset(1);
           }
       }
   }

   • The check if src.chars().count() > INPUT_SIZE only ensures the user is not giving you more than 200 Unicode scalar values.  
   • However, each “char” in Rust occupies four bytes (char is 32-bit in Rust). Yet dest is [u8; INPUT_SIZE], i.e., 200 bytes total.  
   • Writing 200 × 4 bytes = 800 bytes into a 200-byte array leads to out‐of‐bounds writes.

2) Consequences of the Out‐Of‐Bounds Write:
   Writing beyond the end of dest can overwrite nearby variables on the stack. In this program, one of the most interesting variables is login_pin (holding 0x11223344 by default). If an attacker overwrites that memory with 123456, the config panel check will pass. Similarly, if you happened to reorder variables or embed PIN_ENTRY_ENABLED nearby, that too could be overwritten.  

3) How This Lets You Circumvent the PIN Check:
   By feeding a carefully sized string that includes binary data (or carefully chosen Unicode code points), an attacker could set login_pin to 123456 in memory before present_config_panel() reads it. When present_config_panel() does if *pin != 123456, that check would pass, and the shell command is launched.

4) How to Fix:
   • Don’t cast [u8; N] → *mut char. Either store bytes (using .bytes()) or store char values in a [char; N] buffer.  
   • In general, copying string data should either use safe Rust string types or at least be very explicit about size in bytes vs. size in characters.  
   • Validate input lengths carefully and do not allow an out‐of‐range write.  

In other words, yes—thanks to that memory‐corruption opportunity from mixing up bytes and chars, an attacker can overwrite login_pin (or other memory), bypassing the disabled PIN check.

Below is an example of a simplified Matlab Live Script that demonstrates how one might model and visualize wind‐generated water waves in a small rectangular tank (0.42 m × 0.36 m) with uniform wind speed 2.5 m/s. This example uses a very idealized 2D shallow‐water approximation with a constant water depth. In reality, wind‐wave generation is considerably more complex and would typically require advanced CFD or dedicated wave modeling software; nevertheless, this script can serve as a starting educational demonstration.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% Wind-Driven Water Wave Simulation in a Rectangular Tank
%
% This is a simplified 2D shallow-water model that shows wind forcing over a
% rectangular domain. We assume the water depth is constant and small wave
% slopes. In reality, wind-wave generation is more complex, and professional
% CFD codes or specialized wave models (e.g., SPH, VOF, or BEM-based approaches)
% are often used. This script is meant purely as a didactic example.

%% Parameters and Grid Setup
clear; close all; clc;

% Physical parameters
Lx = 0.42;            % Tank length (m)
Ly = 0.36;            % Tank width  (m)
Nx = 50;              % Number of grid points in x-direction
Ny = 50;              % Number of grid points in y-direction
dx = Lx/(Nx-1); 
dy = Ly/(Ny-1);

g = 9.81;             % Gravitational acceleration (m/s^2)
H0 = 0.05;            % Still water depth (m) - assumed constant
rho_water = 1000;     % Density of water (kg/m^3)
rho_air = 1.225;      % Density of air   (kg/m^3)
Cd = 1.0e-3;          % Drag coefficient (order of 1e-3)
U_wind = 2.5;         % Wind speed (m/s)

% Derived wind stress (assuming wind blows along +x direction)
tau_x = rho_air * Cd * U_wind^2;   % (N/m^2)
tau_y = 0.0;                       % No wind in y-direction in this example

% Time stepping
CFL = 0.5;                         % CFL stability parameter
c = sqrt(g*H0);                    % Characteristic wave speed (shallow water)
dt = min(dx,dy)/c * CFL;          % Time step
t_final = 2.0;                     % Final simulation time (s)
nsteps = floor(t_final / dt);

% Create coordinate arrays
x = linspace(0, Lx, Nx); 
y = linspace(0, Ly, Ny);

%% Initialize State Variables
% We will use a linearized shallow water approach where:
%   eta(x,y,t)   = free surface elevation (relative to H0)
%   u(x,y,t)     = velocity in x
%   v(x,y,t)     = velocity in y
% The linearized continuity and momentum equations are:
%   deta/dt + H0*(du/dx + dv/dy) = 0
%   du/dt + g*deta/dx = tau_x/(rho_water*H0)
%   dv/dt + g*deta/dy = tau_y/(rho_water*H0)

eta = zeros(Ny, Nx);  
u   = zeros(Ny, Nx);  
v   = zeros(Ny, Nx);

% Optionally, we can add a small initial perturbation:
for j = 1:Ny
    for i = 1:Nx
        % A small Gaussian bump at center (optional)
        xc = Lx/2;  yc = Ly/2;
        r2 = ((x(i)-xc)/0.05)^2 + ((y(j)-yc)/0.05)^2;
        eta(j,i) = 0.001*exp(-r2);
    end
end

%% Prepare for Visualization
fig = figure('Position',[100 100 800 600]);
ax = axes('Parent',fig);
surfPlot = surf(ax, x, y, eta, 'EdgeColor','none');
axis([0 Lx 0 Ly -0.002 0.002]);
caxis([-0.001 0.001]);
colormap jet;
colorbar;
xlabel('x (m)'); ylabel('y (m)'); zlabel('\eta (m)');
title('Water Surface Elevation');
view(45,30);
drawnow;

%% Time Integration Loop
for step = 1:nsteps

    % ---- 1) Update velocities u and v using shallow water momentum ----
    % Central differences in space, forward Euler in time
    %   du/dt = -g d(eta)/dx + tau_x / (rho_water * H0)
    %   dv/dt = -g d(eta)/dy + tau_y / (rho_water * H0)

    % Create temporary copies to avoid mixing updates within the same step
    u_new = u;
    v_new = v;

    for j = 2:Ny-1
        for i = 2:Nx-1
            d_eta_dx = (eta(j,i+1) - eta(j,i-1)) / (2*dx);
            d_eta_dy = (eta(j+1,i) - eta(j-1,i)) / (2*dy);

            u_new(j,i) = u(j,i) - dt*g*d_eta_dx + ...
                         dt*( tau_x/(rho_water*H0) );
            v_new(j,i) = v(j,i) - dt*g*d_eta_dy + ...
                         dt*( tau_y/(rho_water*H0) );
        end
    end

    % Boundary conditions (simple reflection at walls: u=0, v=0)
    u_new([1 end],:) = 0;  u_new(:,[1 end]) = 0;
    v_new([1 end],:) = 0;  v_new(:,[1 end]) = 0;

    % Update velocities in domain
    u = u_new;
    v = v_new;

    % ---- 2) Update free surface eta using continuity equation ----
    %   deta/dt = -H0 ( d(u)/dx + d(v)/dy )

    eta_new = eta;
    for j = 2:Ny-1
        for i = 2:Nx-1
            d_u_dx = (u(j,i+1) - u(j,i-1)) / (2*dx);
            d_v_dy = (v(j+1,i) - v(j-1,i)) / (2*dy);

            eta_new(j,i) = eta(j,i) - dt*H0 * (d_u_dx + d_v_dy);
        end
    end

    % Enforce no-normal-flow boundary for eta (simple reflection):
    eta_new([1 end],:) = 0; 
    eta_new(:,[1 end]) = 0;

    % Update eta
    eta = eta_new;

    % ---- 3) Visualization/Animation ----
    if mod(step,10)==0 || step == nsteps
        set(surfPlot, 'ZData', eta);
        title(sprintf('Time = %.3f s', step*dt));
        drawnow;
    end
end

%% Final Notes
%
% This simulation heavily simplifies real wind-wave generation. Real physics
% includes turbulence in both air and water, wave breaking, nonlinearity,
% variable water depth, and more. However, this example provides a
% starting point for shallow-wave or PDE-based experiments in Matlab.

Below is an example JavaScript function that takes two inputs:  
1) a JSON object (or JavaScript object parsed from JSON), and  
2) the entity key you want to search for.  

The function recursively searches through the object to find all instances of that key. For each occurrence, it captures both the path to that key (within the object hierarchy) and its value. Finally, it returns an array of these path-value mappings.

Example Usage:  
const sampleJson = {  
  user: {  
    name: "Alice",  
    address: {  
      city: "Wonderland",  
      zip: 12345  
    }  
  },  
  info: {  
    name: "ProjectX",  
    details: {  
      version: "1.0"  
    }  
  }  
};  

findEntity(sampleJson, "name");  
// Output might be:  
// [  
//   { path: "user.name", value: "Alice" },  
//   { path: "info.name", value: "ProjectX" }  
// ]

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

function findEntity(jsonObj, targetKey) {
  const results = [];

  function recurse(obj, currentPath) {
    // Iterate over all keys of the current object
    for (const key in obj) {
      // Build new path for this key
      const newPath = currentPath ? `${currentPath}.${key}` : key;

      // If we find the target key, add its path and value to results
      if (key === targetKey) {
        results.push({
          path: newPath,
          value: obj[key]
        });
      }

      // If the value is an object, recurse into it
      if (obj[key] !== null && typeof obj[key] === 'object') {
        recurse(obj[key], newPath);
      }
    }
  }

  recurse(jsonObj, '');
  return results;
}

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Explanation:
1. Define an empty array results to store the search matches.  
2. recurse is a helper function that:  
   • Loops through all keys of the current object.  
   • Checks if the current key matches targetKey; if so, it saves path and value.  
   • If the current key’s value is another object (and not null), recurse further into it with the updated path.  
3. Finally, findEntity returns the results array containing all matches.  

If you want to input a JSON file as text, remember to parse it before calling findEntity, for example:  
const jsonObj = JSON.parse(jsonString);  
const matches = findEntity(jsonObj, "someKey");

#!/usr/bin/env perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use Cwd qw(abs_path);
use File::Basename qw(basename);
use Getopt::Long;

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reimplementation of the provided Bash script in Perl, preserving:
#   1) Argument parsing (quality, preset, resize, noise, input_file)
#   2) Same external calls (av1an, ffmpeg, rm, exa, video-compare)
#   3) Similar logic and variable handling
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# "Strict" and "warnings" in Perl roughly parallel "set -euo pipefail" behavior.
# We forcibly exit on errors and keep track of uninitialized variables.

# A small helper function, replacing the "get-filename" from video.sh.
# Assumes "get-filename" returned the basename minus extension.
sub get_filename {
    my ($path) = @_;
    my $b = basename($path);
    $b =~ s/\.[^.]+$//;  # remove extension
    return $b;
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Parse command-line arguments
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
my $quality;
my $preset;
my $resize;
my $noise;
GetOptions(
    "q|quality=s" => \$quality,
    "p|preset=s"  => \$preset,
    "r|resize=s"  => \$resize,
    "n|noise=s"   => \$noise,
) or die "Error in command line arguments.\n";

# The remaining argument, if present, should be the input file
my $input_file = shift @ARGV;

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Validate arguments
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
if ( !defined $input_file || !-f $input_file ) {
    # Even if the script continues, note if the file doesn't exist
    warn "FILE NOT FOUND: $input_file\n" if defined $input_file;
}

if ( !defined $quality || !defined $preset || !defined $input_file ) {
    die <<"USAGE";
Usage: $0 
   -q|--quality <quality> 
   -p|--preset <preset> 
   [-n|--noise <noise_level>] 
   [-r|--resize <size>] 
   <input_file>
USAGE
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Derive file paths and names
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
my $file_path = abs_path($input_file);
my $file_name = get_filename($file_path);
my $chunk_method = "ffms2";

my $output_name = "${file_name}.reencode.av1an.aom.rf${quality}.p${preset}";
my $metadata_comment = "reencoded with av1an(aom), 10-bit, cpu-used=${preset}, "
  . "cq=${quality}, chunk-method=$chunk_method";

# If noise was specified
if (defined $noise && $noise ne '') {
    $output_name .= ".noise$noise";
} else {
    $noise = 10;
}

# If resize was specified
if (defined $resize && $resize ne '') {
    $output_name .= ".${resize}p";
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Adjust system limits (ulimit -n 2048).
# Note: this may or may not have an effect depending on your system and shell.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Attempting to replicate "ulimit -n 2048" as in the Bash script.
# Because "ulimit" is often a shell builtin, this may not persist across calls,
# but we attempt it for fidelity.
system("ulimit -n 2048");

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Run av1an
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
my $threads = 2;
my $maybe_scale = '';
if (defined $resize && $resize ne '') {
    # e.g. "-vf 'scale=-1:720'"
    $maybe_scale = " -vf 'scale=-1:$resize'";
}

my $av1an_cmd = join(' ',
    'av1an --verbose',
    "-i \"$file_path\"",
    '--encoder aom',
    '--workers 4',
    '--resume',
    '--extra-split 300',
    '--ignore-frame-mismatch',
    '--audio-params " -an "',
    "--ffmpeg \" -an$maybe_scale\"",
    "--split-method av-scenechange",
    "--chunk-method $chunk_method",
    '--concat mkvmerge',
    "--set-thread-affinity=\"$threads\"",
    "--photon-noise=\"$noise\"",
    '--video-params "',
    '--bit-depth=10',
    "--threads=$threads",
    "--end-usage=q --cq-level=$quality --cpu-used=$preset",
    '--tile-columns=0 --tile-rows=0',
    '--tune-content=psy --tune=ssim',
    '--lag-in-frames=64',
    '--enable-keyframe-filtering=1 --disable-kf --kf-max-dist=9999',
    '--enable-qm=1 --deltaq-mode=0 --aq-mode=0 --quant-b-adapt=1',
    '--enable-fwd-kf=0 --arnr-strength=4 --sb-size=dynamic --enable-dnl-denoising=0',
    '"',
    "-o \"${output_name}.audioless.mkv\""
);

system($av1an_cmd) == 0
  or die "Failed to run av1an command:\n$av1an_cmd\nError: $?\n";

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mux the audio back in and convert to mp4
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
my $ffmpeg_cmd = join(' ',
    'ffmpeg -loglevel warning -hide_banner',
    "-i \"${output_name}.audioless.mkv\"",
    "-i \"$file_path\"",
    "-c copy -map 0:v -map 1:a",
    "-metadata comment=\"$metadata_comment\"",
    "\"${output_name}.mp4\""
);
system($ffmpeg_cmd) == 0
  or die "Failed to run ffmpeg:\n$ffmpeg_cmd\nError: $?\n";

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Remove the temporary audioless file
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
system("rm -rf \"${output_name}.audioless.mkv\"") == 0
  or warn "Could not remove ${output_name}.audioless.mkv\n";

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Show final output via exa
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
my $exa_cmd = join(' ',
    'exa -al --color=always --no-permissions --no-user --time-style=long-iso',
    "\"$input_file\"",
    "\"${output_name}.mp4\""
);
system($exa_cmd);

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Print compare hint
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
print "\nvideo-compare \"$input_file\" \"${output_name}.mp4\"\n";

print "\n✅ FINISHED\n";

Below is an example of a recursive JavaScript function that attempts a request up to three times before either resolving (if successful) or ultimately rejecting. It does not use async/await; it uses raw promises instead:

function requestWithRetries(url, retries = 3) {
  return new Promise(function (resolve, reject) {
    fetch(url)
      .then(function (response) {
        if (!response.ok) {
          // Treat non-2xx status as an error
          throw new Error('Request failed with status ' + response.status);
        }
        resolve(response);
      })
      .catch(function (error) {
        // If we still have retries left, call ourselves again
        if (retries > 1) {
          requestWithRetries(url, retries - 1).then(resolve).catch(reject);
        } else {
          // No retries left; reject the promise
          reject(error);
        }
      });
  });
}

// Example usage:
requestWithRetries('https://api.example.com/data')
  .then(function (response) {
    return response.json();
  })
  .then(function (data) {
    console.log('Successful response data:', data);
  })
  .catch(function (error) {
    console.error('Request failed:', error);
  });

Explanation:
1. requestWithRetries creates a new Promise.
2. It attempts to fetch the given URL. If fetch succeeds and response is OK, it resolves the promise.  
3. If fetch fails or the response is not OK, it checks whether there are retries remaining.  
4. If there are retries left, it calls requestWithRetries again (recursively). If not, it rejects the promise.

