Below is a simple example of how you could implement an iMessage extension (an Messages extension) in Swift that displays two buttons in the keyboard view. This example uses a UIStackView to layout the buttons side by side in the MessagesViewController, which is the main entry point of your iMessage extension.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
import UIKit
import Messages

class MessagesViewController: MSMessagesAppViewController {

    override func viewDidLoad() {
        super.viewDidLoad()

        // Create two buttons
        let button1 = UIButton(type: .system)
        button1.setTitle("Button 1", for: .normal)
        // Add target/action if you want to respond to taps:
        // button1.addTarget(self, action: #selector(button1Tapped), for: .touchUpInside)

        let button2 = UIButton(type: .system)
        button2.setTitle("Button 2", for: .normal)
        // button2.addTarget(self, action: #selector(button2Tapped), for: .touchUpInside)

        // Create a UIStackView to arrange the buttons horizontally
        let stackView = UIStackView(arrangedSubviews: [button1, button2])
        stackView.axis = .horizontal
        stackView.distribution = .fillEqually
        stackView.alignment = .center
        stackView.spacing = 16
        stackView.translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false

        // Add the stackView to the main view
        self.view.addSubview(stackView)

        // Center the stackView in the superview
        NSLayoutConstraint.activate([
            stackView.centerXAnchor.constraint(equalTo: self.view.centerXAnchor),
            stackView.centerYAnchor.constraint(equalTo: self.view.centerYAnchor)
        ])
    }

    // If you'd like to handle button taps:
    /*
    @objc func button1Tapped() {
        // Handle button 1 tap
    }

    @objc func button2Tapped() {
        // Handle button 2 tap
    }
    */
}
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Follow these steps to set up the project:

1. In Xcode, go to File → New → Project, select “iMessage App,” and follow the prompts to create a new Messages extension template project.  
2. Locate the MessagesViewController.swift file (the main view controller for your iMessage extension) and replace or edit the contents with the code above.  
3. Customize the titles and appearances of the buttons as you see fit (e.g., background color, tinted color, fonts).  
4. Add button tap handlers as needed to implement functionality.  
5. Build and run the iMessage extension on a simulator or a real device to see the two buttons displayed.  

When you launch Messages and choose your iMessage extension in the app drawer, the above buttons should appear in the custom keyboard area. By default, the extension is shown in the compact presentation style; you can expand it using the upward arrow if you want more space or to present more content.

Below is a simple way to trigger your browser to download a file (or webpage) from a URL by running a short snippet in the browser’s JavaScript console:

1. Open your browser’s developer tools (usually F12 or right-click → “Inspect”).  
2. Switch to the “Console” tab.  
3. Paste the code below, replacing the placeholder URL with the desired file/page URL, then press Enter:

-----------------------------------------------------------
const url = "https://example.com/page.html"; // Replace with the desired URL

// Create an invisible link
const link = document.createElement('a');
link.href = url;

// Provide a default filename (optional, the server itself may override this)
link.download = "page.html";

// Programmatically click the link to trigger the download
document.body.appendChild(link);
link.click();

// Cleanup
document.body.removeChild(link);
-----------------------------------------------------------

Notes:
• The “download” attribute may be ignored by some browsers if the server does not permit the file to be downloaded, or if you are dealing with cross-origin URLs that do not allow CORS.  
• If the direct “download” attribute is disallowed by the server headers or cross-origin policies, you may need to fetch the content as a Blob, then use the blob URL to force a download. Let me know if you need that approach!

Below is an example Rust program that reads URLs from a file (named "urls.txt" by default), separates YouTube URLs from others, and downloads the YouTube URLs using yt-dlp. Make sure you have yt-dlp installed and available on your system's PATH, and adjust the code as needed (e.g., error handling, file paths, etc.) for production use.

----------------------------------------
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{BufRead, BufReader};
use std::process::Command;

fn main() -> std::io::Result<()> {
    // Replace "urls.txt" with the actual path to your file if needed
    let file = File::open("urls.txt")?;
    let reader = BufReader::new(file);

    let mut youtube_urls = Vec::new();
    let mut other_urls = Vec::new();

    // Read the file line by line
    for line_result in reader.lines() {
        let url = line_result?;
        // You can tweak the detection logic for YouTube URLs if desired
        if url.contains("youtube.com") || url.contains("youtu.be") {
            youtube_urls.push(url);
        } else {
            other_urls.push(url);
        }
    }

    println!("YouTube URLs:");
    for url in &youtube_urls {
        println!("{}", url);
    }

    println!("\nOther URLs:");
    for url in &other_urls {
        println!("{}", url);
    }

    // Download the YouTube URLs using yt-dlp
    // This will spawn a yt-dlp process for each URL
    for url in youtube_urls {
        let status = Command::new("yt-dlp")
            .arg(&url)
            .status()
            .unwrap_or_else(|_| {
                panic!("Failed to execute yt-dlp for URL: {}", url);
            });

        if !status.success() {
            eprintln!("yt-dlp failed when processing {}", url);
        }
    }

    Ok(())
}
----------------------------------------

How it works:
1. Opens the file "urls.txt" using std::fs::File and wraps it in a BufReader for efficient line-by-line reading.
2. Iterates through each line, classifying the URL into a youtube_urls vector if it matches "youtube.com" or "youtu.be" and otherwise into other_urls.
3. Prints the collected YouTube and other URLs for demonstration.  
4. Iterates over youtube_urls to call yt-dlp for each URL using Command::new("yt-dlp").arg(&url).  
5. You can modify urls.txt, the detection logic, or the code as appropriate for your use case!

Below is a simple example of using HttpClient to send a POST request with a client certificate. The sample reads a .pfx certificate file from disk (with its associated password), attaches it to an HttpClientHandler, and then sends a JSON payload to a target URL. Adjust the certificate path, password, and the URL as necessary for your environment.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Program.cs
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;

namespace HttpClientWithCert
{
    class Program
    {
        static async Task Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Path to your .pfx certificate file
            string certPath = "path/to/your/certificate.pfx";
            // Password for the .pfx file
            string certPassword = "your_certificate_password";

            // Load the client certificate
            X509Certificate2 clientCertificate = new X509Certificate2(certPath, certPassword);

            // Create an HttpClientHandler and add the certificate
            HttpClientHandler handler = new HttpClientHandler();
            handler.ClientCertificates.Add(clientCertificate);

            // Optionally, you can configure custom server certificate validation
            // (e.g., ignore self-signed certificates). Use carefully in production.
            // handler.ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback = (sender, cert, chain, sslPolicyErrors) => true;

            // Create an HttpClient using the handler
            using (HttpClient client = new HttpClient(handler))
            {
                // Example JSON content
                string jsonData = "{\"exampleKey\":\"exampleValue\"}";
                HttpContent content = new StringContent(jsonData, Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");

                // The URL you want to send the POST to
                string requestUri = "https://example.com/api/endpoint";

                try
                {
                    // Send the POST request
                    HttpResponseMessage response = await client.PostAsync(requestUri, content);

                    // Read the response (for demonstration)
                    string responseBody = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
                    Console.WriteLine("Status code: " + response.StatusCode);
                    Console.WriteLine("Response body: " + responseBody);
                }
                catch (Exception ex)
                {
                    Console.WriteLine("Error: " + ex.Message);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Explanation:
1. X509Certificate2 is used to load your .pfx file and set the client certificate.
2. HttpClientHandler.ClientCertificates.Add(...) attaches the loaded certificate to the HttpClient.
3. The POST request is sent via HttpClient.PostAsync(...). 
4. Adjust paths, password, and URIs to match your specific use case.

Below is a simple example of how you can use regular expressions in C# to extract hashtags from a piece of text. In this example, the pattern #\w+ matches a hash sign (#) followed by one or more word characters (letters, digits, or underscores).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Example: Extracting hashtags in C#

using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        string text = "Hello #World! Welcome to #CSharp. Let's use #Regex for #100DaysOfCode.";

        List<string> hashtags = ExtractHashtags(text);

        Console.WriteLine("Extracted hashtags:");
        foreach (var tag in hashtags)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(tag);
        }
    }

    public static List<string> ExtractHashtags(string input)
    {
        // Regex pattern: '#' followed by one or more word characters
        Regex hashtagPattern = new Regex(@"#\w+");

        // Find all matches in the input string
        MatchCollection matches = hashtagPattern.Matches(input);

        // Convert matches to a List<string>
        return matches.Cast<Match>().Select(m => m.Value).ToList();
    }
}
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Explanation:
• @"#\w+" is a regular expression that requires a '#' character followed by one or more "word characters", which include letters, digits, and underscores.  
• Regex.Matches will find all substrings in your input that match this pattern.  
• The result is then converted to a List<string> for easy access to each match.  

You can adjust the pattern if you want to allow additional characters (such as hyphens) inside your hashtags or limit to only alphabetical characters.

