Jetpack Compose doesn’t directly have a concept called “container queries” like in CSS on the web, but you can achieve a similar effect by using a few different techniques. The most common approach is to use BoxWithConstraints (or a custom layout) so that you can condition your layout based on the space available from the parent.

Below is a basic example of how you can use BoxWithConstraints to change how content is laid out depending on the parent’s size. You can think of this as “container queries” for Jetpack Compose:

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@Composable
fun AdaptiveComposable(
    modifier: Modifier = Modifier
) {
    // BoxWithConstraints lets us retrieve the constraints of its parent,
    // specifically `maxWidth` and `maxHeight`.
    BoxWithConstraints(modifier = modifier) {
        // Here we can decide on thresholds (in dp) that fit our UI’s needs.
        // e.g., Maybe we want a "compact" layout when maxWidth < 400.dp.
        val isCompact = maxWidth < 400.dp

        if (isCompact) {
            // For smaller container sizes, stack items vertically, or
            // show a simpler layout, or show simplified content.
            Column(
                modifier = Modifier.fillMaxSize(),
                verticalArrangement = Arrangement.Center,
                horizontalAlignment = Alignment.CenterHorizontally
            ) {
                Text(text = "Compact Mode")
                Spacer(modifier = Modifier.height(8.dp))
                Button(onClick = { /* ... */ }) {
                    Text("Action")
                }
            }
        } else {
            // For larger container sizes, maybe place items side by side, or
            // display additional content.
            Row(
                modifier = Modifier.fillMaxSize(),
                verticalAlignment = Alignment.CenterVertically,
                horizontalArrangement = Arrangement.SpaceEvenly
            ) {
                Text(text = "Expanded Mode")
                Button(onClick = { /* ... */ }) {
                    Text("Action")
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Explanation:

• BoxWithConstraints: This composable gives you access to the constraints provided by its parent. In particular, you have maxWidth, minWidth, maxHeight, and minHeight. You can use these to decide which layout variant to show.

• Conditional Layout: Inside BoxWithConstraints, you can conditionally render different composables depending on the constraints (for example, isCompact in the snippet).

• Layout Variation: For a small container, you might stack elements in a Column or simplify the UI; for a larger container, you can use a Row or show more details.

Beyond BoxWithConstraints (and the if/else approach), you can create fully custom layouts that measure children differently based on available space. However, for most use cases that mimic responsive or container-query-like patterns, BoxWithConstraints is the most straightforward and readable solution.
